window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-phy-solids","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Mechanical Properties of Solids · Practice","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","subject":"Physics","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":1,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A 5.0 kg mass is tied to one end of a steel wire. The mass is whirled in a vertical circle of radius 0.50 m (take the radius as the unstretched length of the wire). At the lowest point, its angular velocity is 10 rad s$^{-1}$. The wire has a cross-sectional area of $2.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$ and $Y = 2.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$. What is the elongation of the wire at the lowest point? (g = 10 m s$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["0.375 mm","0.313 mm","0.250 mm","0.0625 mm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At the lowest point, the tension must support the weight and also provide the centripetal force: $T = mg + m\\omega^2 r$.\nSubstituting: T = 5 × 10 + 5 × 100 × 0.5 = 50 + 250 = 300 N.\nFormula: $\\Delta L = \\frac{TL}{AY} = \\frac{300 \\times 0.5}{2.0 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 2.0 \\times 10^{11}}$.\nSo $\\Delta L = 3.75 \\times 10^{-4}$ m = 0.375 mm.\nOption (3) wrongly uses $m\\omega^2 r - mg$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":2,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"For a wire stretched within its proportional limit, the stress–strain graph is a straight line through the origin. What does the area under this line, from the origin up to a given strain, represent?","image":"","options":["Young's modulus of the material","Total elastic potential energy stored in the whole wire","Elastic potential energy stored per unit volume of the wire","Yield strength of the material"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In the linear region, stress rises uniformly from 0 to $\\sigma$ as strain goes from 0 to $\\varepsilon$.\nThe area under this line is a triangle: $\\frac{1}{2}\\sigma\\varepsilon$. This is exactly the energy density $u$.\nThe slope of the line gives Young's modulus, not the area.\nThe area is energy per unit volume, not the total energy. For the total, multiply by the volume $AL$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":3,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"The stress-strain curve of a metal wire is a straight line from the origin up to point A, where the stress is $2.0 \\times 10^{8}\\,N\\,m^{-2}$ and the strain is $1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$. A wire of this metal is 2.5 m long and has a cross-sectional area of $1.5\\,mm^2$. A 24 kg mass is hung from it. Find the elongation of the wire. (Take $g = 10\\,m/s^2$.)","image":"","options":["1.6 mm","2.0 mm","2.5 mm","0.2 mm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: from the linear region OA, $Y = \\frac{2.0 \\times 10^{8}}{1.0 \\times 10^{-3}} = 2.0 \\times 10^{11}$ Pa. \nStress: $\\frac{mg}{A} = \\frac{240}{1.5 \\times 10^{-6}} = 1.6 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa. This is below the stress at A, so Hooke's law applies. \nFormula: $\\Delta L = \\frac{\\text{stress} \\times L}{Y}$ \nSubstituting: $\\Delta L = \\frac{1.6 \\times 10^{8} \\times 2.5}{2.0 \\times 10^{11}} = 2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m \nSo the elongation is 2.0 mm. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":4,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Steel is preferred over copper, brass and aluminium in heavy-duty machines and structural designs.\nReason (R): For wires of the same cross-section, steel needs a much larger force than these metals to produce the same strain.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that steel is preferred in heavy-duty machines and structural designs.\nR is true: a 0.1% strain in a 0.1 cm$^2$ wire needs 2000 N for steel, but only 690 N, 900 N and 1100 N for aluminium, brass and copper.\nR explains A: steel has the larger Young's modulus, so it deforms the least under heavy loads.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":5,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A rectangular metal block has a top face of 40 cm $\\times$ 20 cm and a height of 5 cm. Its bottom face is riveted to the floor. A tangential force of $4.0 \\times 10^{4}$ N is applied on the top face, parallel to it. What is the shearing stress on the block?","image":"","options":["$2.0 \\times 10^{6}$ Pa","$5.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa","$4.0 \\times 10^{6}$ Pa","$2.5 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: F = $4.0 \\times 10^4$ N. The force acts parallel to the top face, so the relevant area is A = 0.40 m $\\times$ 0.20 m = 0.08 m$^2$.\nFormula: shearing stress = $\\frac{F}{A}$, where A is the face parallel to the force.\nSubstituting: stress = $\\frac{4.0 \\times 10^4}{0.08}$.\nSo shearing stress = $5.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa. Using a side face of area 0.02 m$^2$ would wrongly give $2.0 \\times 10^6$ Pa.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":6,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A brass block has its lower face fixed. A tangential force on its upper face produces a shearing stress of $7.2 \\times 10^{6}$ N m$^{-2}$. The shear modulus of brass is 36 GPa. What is the angle of shear $\\theta$?","image":"","options":["$2.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ rad","$5.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ rad","$0.5 \\times 10^{-4}$ rad","$20 \\times 10^{-4}$ rad"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\sigma_s = 7.2 \\times 10^{6}$ Pa and $G = 36 \\times 10^{9}$ Pa.\nFormula: $\\sigma_s = G\\theta$, so $\\theta = \\frac{\\sigma_s}{G}$.\nSubstituting: $\\theta = \\frac{7.2 \\times 10^{6}}{36 \\times 10^{9}} = 2.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ rad.\nSo the angle of shear is $2.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ rad.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":7,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"For the typical stress-strain curve of a metal (O is the origin, then points A, B, D and E in order of increasing strain), match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Region OA\n(B) Point B\n(C) Point D\n(D) Point E\nList-II:\n(i) Fracture\n(ii) Ultimate tensile strength\n(iii) Yield point (elastic limit)\n(iv) Hooke's law is obeyed","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"OA: the curve is linear here, so stress is proportional to strain and Hooke's law holds → (iv).\nB: this is the yield point, also called the elastic limit. The stress here is the yield strength → (iii).\nD: this is the maximum stress the material can bear, the ultimate tensile strength → (ii).\nE: the material breaks here, which is fracture → (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":8,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"The lower face of a cube of side 10 cm is fixed. A tangential force on the upper face displaces it by 0.02 mm relative to the lower face. What is the shearing strain?","image":"","options":["$2 \\times 10^{-3}$","$2 \\times 10^{-6}$","$2 \\times 10^{-5}$","$2 \\times 10^{-4}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta x = 0.02$ mm $= 2 \\times 10^{-5}$ m, $L = 10$ cm $= 0.1$ m.\nFormula: shearing strain $= \\frac{\\Delta x}{L}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{2 \\times 10^{-5}}{0.1} = 2 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nSo the shearing strain is $2 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":9,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"What is the dimensional formula of stress?","image":"","options":["$[MLT^{-2}]$","$[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$","$[ML^{2}T^{-2}]$","$[M^0L^0T^0]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Stress = force/area = $\\frac{[MLT^{-2}]}{[L^2]}$.\nThis gives $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$, the same as pressure.\n$[MLT^{-2}]$ is force, not force per unit area. $[ML^2T^{-2}]$ is energy. $[M^0L^0T^0]$ is strain, which is dimensionless.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":10,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Tensile and compressive stresses are both called longitudinal stress.\n(ii) Under hydraulic stress, a body changes both its volume and its shape.\n(iii) The restoring force developed in a deformed body in equilibrium is equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to the applied force.\n(iv) Shearing stress can be sustained by liquids as well as solids.\n(v) Stress is defined as the restoring force per unit area.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: both tensile and compressive stress change the length, so both are longitudinal.\n(ii) Incorrect: only the volume changes; the shape stays the same.\n(iii) Correct: the restoring force balances the applied force.\n(iv) Incorrect: shearing stress is possible only in solids.\n(v) Correct: this is the definition of stress.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":11,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (design feature) with List-II (reason):\nList-I\n(A) Crane rope radius chosen about 3 cm instead of about 1 cm\n(B) Rope made of many thin braided wires\n(C) I-shaped cross-section of load-bearing beams\n(D) Height of mountains on Earth limited to about 10 km\nList-II\n(i) Gives a large load-bearing surface and enough depth, avoids buckling and reduces weight\n(ii) Shear stress at the base must stay below the elastic limit of rock\n(iii) Safety margin of about a factor of ten in the load\n(iv) Flexibility and ease of manufacture","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-i, B-iv, C-iii, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The minimum radius is about 1 cm. A factor-of-ten safety margin on the load raises it to about 3 cm, so A-iii.\n(B) Braided thin wires give flexibility, ease of manufacture and strength, so B-iv.\n(C) The I-section gives load-bearing surface and depth, prevents buckling and saves weight, so C-i.\n(D) The condition $h\\rho g$ < elastic limit of rock limits the height, so D-ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":12,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation) with List-II (key condition used to solve it).\nList-I:\n(P) Copper and steel wires joined end to end, carrying a load\n(Q) A light rigid bar hung by three equal-length wires stays horizontal\n(R) Identical hollow columns sharing a load uniformly\n(S) A cable whose stress must not exceed a limiting value\nList-II:\n(1) Load-bearing area is $\\pi(r_2^2 - r_1^2)$\n(2) Same tension in each part\n(3) Maximum load equals the limiting stress times the area\n(4) Equal elongation in every wire","image":"","options":["P-4, Q-2, R-1, S-3","P-2, Q-1, R-4, S-3","P-2, Q-4, R-1, S-3","P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(P)–(2): Wires in series each transmit the full load, so the tension is the same in both.\n(Q)–(4): A bar that stays horizontal means every wire stretches by the same amount.\n(R)–(1): A hollow column bears the load only over the ring area $\\pi(r_2^2 - r_1^2)$.\n(S)–(3): $F_{max} = \\sigma_{limit} \\times A$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":13,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about bulk modulus and compressibility is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The bulk moduli of solids are much larger than those of liquids, which in turn are much larger than that of air","Compressibility is the fractional change in volume per unit increase in pressure","The negative sign in $B = -\\frac{p}{\\Delta V/V}$ makes the bulk modulus negative for a body being compressed","Hydraulic stress changes the volume of a body without changing its shape"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT's table shows B(solids) ≫ B(liquids) ≫ B(gases).\n(2) Correct: $k = 1/B = -\\frac{1}{\\Delta p}\\frac{\\Delta V}{V}$.\n(3) Incorrect: a positive p produces a negative $\\Delta V$. The minus sign is there precisely so that B is always positive.\n(4) Correct: a uniform normal force on every point of the surface gives a volume change with no change in shape.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":14,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A sample of air in a closed container is compressed by applying pressure. Which elastic modulus can meaningfully describe this deformation?","image":"","options":["Young's modulus","Shear modulus","Bulk modulus","Both Young's modulus and shear modulus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Young's modulus deals with change in length, and shear modulus deals with change in shape.\nBoth need the body to have a definite length and shape, which only solids have.\nA gas has no fixed shape, but its volume changes under pressure.\nBulk modulus $B = -\\frac{p}{\\Delta V/V}$ describes volume change, so it applies to solids, liquids and gases.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":15,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel wire is 2 m long and has a cross-sectional area of 1 mm$^2$. Its Young's modulus is $2 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$. It is slowly stretched by a force of 200 N, within its elastic limit. What elastic potential energy is stored in the wire?","image":"","options":["0.4 J","0.2 J","2.0 J","0.1 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $L = 2$ m, $A = 10^{-6}$ m$^2$, $Y = 2 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$, $F = 200$ N.\nExtension: $l = \\frac{FL}{YA} = \\frac{200 \\times 2}{2 \\times 10^{11} \\times 10^{-6}} = 2 \\times 10^{-3}$ m.\nFormula: $U = \\frac{1}{2}Fl$\nSubstituting: $U = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 200 \\times 2 \\times 10^{-3}$\nSo $U = 0.2$ J. Using $Fl$ without the half gives 0.4 J.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":16,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (material) with List-II (approximate bulk modulus, as given in NCERT Table 8.3).\nList-I:\n(a) Steel\n(b) Water\n(c) Air at STP\n(d) Mercury\nList-II:\n(i) 25 GPa\n(ii) $1.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ GPa\n(iii) 160 GPa\n(iv) 2.2 GPa","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Steel is a solid with tightly coupled atoms, so it has the largest value: 160 GPa, giving (iii).\n(b) Water is a liquid: 2.2 GPa, giving (iv).\n(c) Air is a gas and is extremely compressible: $1.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ GPa, giving (ii).\n(d) Mercury is a liquid with an unusually large bulk modulus: 25 GPa, giving (i).\nThe order solids > liquids > gases holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":17,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"On a hypothetical planet, the acceleration due to gravity is 4 m/s$^2$. The rocks there have density $2.5 \\times 10^3$ kg/m$^3$, and their elastic limit is $30 \\times 10^7$ N m$^{-2}$. Use NCERT's reasoning for the maximum height of mountains, where the shear component of stress at the base is about $h\\rho g$. What is the approximate maximum height of a mountain on this planet?","image":"","options":["12 km","30 km","75 km","3 km"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: g = 4 m/s$^2$, $\\rho = 2.5 \\times 10^3$ kg/m$^3$, elastic limit = $3 \\times 10^8$ N/m$^2$.\nIdea: the sides of the mountain are free, so the base is not under bulk compression. The rocks start to flow when the shear component, about $h\\rho g$, reaches the elastic limit.\nFormula: $h = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\rho g}$.\nSubstituting: $h = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8}{2.5 \\times 10^3 \\times 4} = 3 \\times 10^4$ m.\nSo h ≈ 30 km.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":18,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A square brass slab has side 40 cm and thickness 8 cm. Its lower edge is riveted to the floor. A shearing force of $7.2 \\times 10^{4}$ N acts parallel to its upper narrow face, which measures 40 cm × 8 cm. The shear modulus of brass is 36 GPa. By how much is the upper edge displaced?","image":"","options":["0.005 mm","0.025 mm","0.25 mm","0.0125 mm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: the face area parallel to the force is $A = 0.40 \\times 0.08 = 0.032$ m². The height perpendicular to the force is $L = 0.40$ m.\nStress: $\\frac{F}{A} = \\frac{7.2 \\times 10^{4}}{0.032} = 2.25 \\times 10^{6}$ Pa.\nFormula: $\\Delta x = \\frac{\\text{stress} \\times L}{G}$\nSubstituting: $\\Delta x = \\frac{2.25 \\times 10^{6} \\times 0.40}{36 \\times 10^{9}} = 2.5 \\times 10^{-5}$ m.\nSo $\\Delta x$ = 0.025 mm. Using L = 0.08 m instead would wrongly give 0.005 mm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":19,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"On the stress-strain curve of a metal, the stress corresponding to the point beyond which additional strain is produced even by a reduced applied force is called the","image":"","options":["yield strength","ultimate tensile strength","breaking stress at fracture","proportional limit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Point D is the highest point of the curve. The stress there is the ultimate tensile strength $\\sigma_u$.\nBeyond D, extra strain is produced even when the applied force is reduced, and fracture finally occurs at E.\nYield strength $\\sigma_y$ belongs to point B, the elastic limit, not to D.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":20,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel wire of length 1.5 m and a copper wire of length 1.1 m have the same diameter. They are joined end to end and stretched by a load, and the total elongation is 0.70 mm. Take $Y_{steel} = 2.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$ and $Y_{copper} = 1.1 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$. What is the elongation of the steel wire?","image":"","options":["0.30 mm","0.40 mm","0.35 mm","0.25 mm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: both wires carry the same tension and have the same area, so they have equal stress.\nFormula: $\\frac{\\Delta L_c}{\\Delta L_s} = \\frac{Y_s}{Y_c} \\times \\frac{L_c}{L_s}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{\\Delta L_c}{\\Delta L_s} = \\frac{2.0}{1.1} \\times \\frac{1.1}{1.5} = \\frac{4}{3}$, and $\\Delta L_s + \\Delta L_c = 0.70$ mm.\nSo $\\Delta L_s = \\frac{0.70}{1 + 4/3} = 0.30$ mm, which makes $\\Delta L_c = 0.40$ mm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":21,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel wire of cross-sectional area 2.0 mm$^2$ and yield strength $2.5 \\times 10^{8}$ N m$^{-2}$ hangs from the ceiling of a lift, with a block attached to its lower end. The lift accelerates upward at 2.5 m s$^{-2}$. What is the maximum mass of the block for which the wire does not acquire a permanent set? (Take g = 10 m s$^{-2}$; neglect the mass of the wire.)","image":"","options":["40 kg","50 kg","66.7 kg","20 kg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A = $2.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$, $\\sigma_y = 2.5 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa, a = 2.5 m s$^{-2}$ upward.\nFormula: no permanent set requires stress ≤ $\\sigma_y$. The tension is T = m(g + a), so the condition is m(g + a) ≤ $\\sigma_y A$.\nSubstituting: $\\sigma_y A$ = 500 N, so m ≤ 500/12.5.\nSo m(max) = 40 kg. Ignoring the acceleration would wrongly give 50 kg.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":22,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Hooke's law is written as stress $= k \\times$ strain. What is the constant $k$ called, and what is its SI unit?","image":"","options":["Compressibility; $m^2\\,N^{-1}$","Modulus of elasticity; $N\\,m^{-2}$ (Pa)","Modulus of elasticity; dimensionless","Yield strength; $N\\,m^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The proportionality constant in Hooke's law is called the modulus of elasticity. \nStrain has no units, so $k$ has the same unit as stress: $N\\,m^{-2}$, or pascal. \nOption (3) is wrong because only the strain is dimensionless, not $k$. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":23,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A light rigid rod 1.5 m long hangs horizontally from two vertical wires of equal length. One wire is attached at each end of the rod. The left wire is steel (diameter 1.0 mm, $Y = 2.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$). The right wire is brass (diameter 2.0 mm, $Y = 1.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$). At what distance from the steel end must a load be hung so that the rod remains horizontal?","image":"","options":["0.50 m","0.75 m","1.0 m","1.2 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The rod stays horizontal only if both wires stretch equally. With equal lengths, $\\Delta L = \\frac{TL}{AY}$ gives $T \\propto AY$.\nThe brass area is 4 times the steel area, so $\\frac{T_s}{T_b} = \\frac{1 \\times 2.0}{4 \\times 1.0} = \\frac{1}{2}$, i.e. $T_b = 2T_s$.\nTaking torques about the load point at distance x from the steel end: $T_s x = T_b (1.5 - x)$, so $x = 2(1.5 - x)$.\nSo x = 1.0 m from the steel end.\nOption (1) inverts the tension ratio.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":24,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"For two wires P and Q within their linear (Hooke's law) region, a graph is plotted with stress on the x-axis and strain on the y-axis. Both graphs are straight lines through the origin, and the line for P is steeper than that for Q. Which statement is correct?","image":"","options":["P has the larger modulus of elasticity","Q has the larger modulus of elasticity","Both have equal moduli, since both lines pass through the origin","The moduli cannot be compared without knowing the yield strengths"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Here the slope = strain/stress = $1/k$, because the axes are swapped compared with the usual graph.\nA steeper line for P means a larger $1/k$, so a smaller modulus for P.\nTherefore Q has the larger modulus. For the same stress, Q shows less strain.\nOption (1) would be right only if stress were plotted on the y-axis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":25,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A wooden beam of rectangular cross-section 4 cm × 2 cm rests on two supports and carries a load at its centre. In arrangement P, the 4 cm side is horizontal (breadth 4 cm, depth 2 cm). In arrangement Q, the beam is turned so that the 4 cm side is vertical (breadth 2 cm, depth 4 cm). The load and span are the same in both. Find the ratio of sag in P to sag in Q.","image":"","options":["4","2","8","16"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: P has b = 4 cm, d = 2 cm. Q has b = 2 cm, d = 4 cm.\nFormula: $\\delta \\propto \\frac{1}{bd^3}$, so $\\frac{\\delta_P}{\\delta_Q} = \\frac{b_Q d_Q^3}{b_P d_P^3}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{2 \\times 4^3}{4 \\times 2^3} = \\frac{128}{32}$.\nSo the ratio = 4. Standing the beam on its narrow edge reduces the sag four times.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":26,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Read the two statements about the elastic tissue of the aorta.\nStatement I: Its elastic region is very large, but it does not obey Hooke's law over most of that region.\nStatement II: Its stress-strain curve has no well-defined plastic region.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. The aorta tissue can take large strains and still recover its shape. However, its stress-strain curve is not linear over most of the elastic range, so Hooke's law does not hold there. \nStatement II: Correct. NCERT notes that this curve has no well-defined plastic region. \nMaterials like this, including rubber, are called elastomers. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":27,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A glass block and a copper block are placed in the same fluid and subjected to the same increase in hydraulic pressure. The glass block has twice the volume of the copper block. Using B(glass) = 37 GPa and B(copper) = 140 GPa, find the ratio of the decrease in volume of the glass block to that of the copper block.","image":"","options":["7.57","3.78","0.53","15.1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the same Δp acts on both blocks, $V_g = 2V_c$, $B_g$ = 37 GPa, $B_c$ = 140 GPa.\nFormula: ΔV = VΔp/B.\nRatio: $\\frac{\\Delta V_g}{\\Delta V_c} = \\frac{V_g}{V_c}\\times\\frac{B_c}{B_g}$ = 2 × 140/37.\nSo the ratio = 7.57.\nOption (2) forgets the factor of 2 for volume, and option (3) inverts the ratio of the moduli.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":28,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about stress-strain behaviour are correct?\n(a) In the region between A and B of a metal's curve, stress is proportional to strain.\n(b) A metal unloaded from a point between A and B regains its original dimensions.\n(c) Between B and D, strain increases rapidly even for a small change in stress.\n(d) Beyond point D, additional strain is produced even by a reduced applied force.\n(e) Aorta tissue obeys Hooke's law over most of its elastic region.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Incorrect: proportionality holds only in OA, not in AB.\n(b) Correct: AB is still within the elastic limit B.\n(c) Correct: beyond the yield strength, strain increases rapidly.\n(d) Correct: after the ultimate strength D, strain grows even with reduced force until fracture at E.\n(e) Incorrect: aorta tissue does not obey Hooke's law over most of its large elastic region.\nCorrect statements: 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":29,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel wire of length 2.0 m and cross-sectional area 2.0 mm$^2$ hangs from a rigid ceiling. A 20 kg block is suspended from its lower end, and the wire stays within the proportional limit. Take $Y_{steel} = 2.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$ and g = 10 m/s$^2$. Find the elastic potential energy stored per unit volume of the wire.","image":"","options":["$2.5 \\times 10^{4}$ J m$^{-3}$","$5.0 \\times 10^{4}$ J m$^{-3}$","$1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ J m$^{-3}$","$1.25 \\times 10^{4}$ J m$^{-3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: F = 200 N, $A = 2.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$. The tension at any section is F, not 2F, even though the ceiling also pulls on the wire.\nStress: $\\sigma = \\frac{200}{2 \\times 10^{-6}} = 1.0 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa.\nStrain: $\\varepsilon = \\frac{\\sigma}{Y} = \\frac{10^8}{2 \\times 10^{11}} = 5 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nFormula: $u = \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma\\varepsilon = \\frac{1}{2}(10^8)(5 \\times 10^{-4})$.\nSo $u = 2.5 \\times 10^{4}$ J m$^{-3}$.\nUsing 2F by mistake would give $1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ J m$^{-3}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":30,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Two wires P and Q are made of the same material and carry the same load. The length of P is twice that of Q, and the diameter of P is half that of Q. What is the ratio of the elongation of P to that of Q?","image":"","options":["8","4","2","16"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $L_P = 2L_Q$, $d_P = d_Q/2$, with the same F and Y.\nFormula: $\\Delta L = \\frac{FL}{AY} \\propto \\frac{L}{d^2}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{\\Delta L_P}{\\Delta L_Q} = \\frac{L_P}{L_Q} \\times \\left(\\frac{d_Q}{d_P}\\right)^2 = 2 \\times 4$.\nSo the ratio = 8.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":31,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a copper wire ($Y = 1.1 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$) and a steel wire ($Y = 2.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$) are under the same tensile stress, the copper wire stores more elastic energy per unit volume.\nReason (R): The elastic energy density of a stretched wire is $\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2Y}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: substitute $\\varepsilon = \\sigma/Y$ into $u = \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma\\varepsilon$ to get $u = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2Y}$.\nA is true: for the same $\\sigma$, $u \\propto 1/Y$. Copper has the smaller $Y$, so it stores more energy per unit volume. It does so because it strains more.\nR directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":32,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"An aluminium block has a square top face of side 10 cm and a height of 4.0 cm. Its lower face is fixed to the floor. A tangential force of $5.0 \\times 10^4$ N acts on the top face. The shear modulus of aluminium is 25 GPa. By how much is the top face displaced relative to the bottom face?","image":"","options":["8 μm","80 μm","2 μm","0.8 μm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $F = 5 \\times 10^4$ N, $A = 0.1 \\times 0.1 = 0.01$ m$^2$, $L = 0.04$ m, $G = 25 \\times 10^9$ Pa.\nFormula: shearing stress $= F/A$; shearing strain $\\theta = \\frac{\\text{stress}}{G}$; $\\Delta x = \\theta L$.\nSubstituting: stress $= 5 \\times 10^6$ Pa, so $\\theta = \\frac{5 \\times 10^6}{25 \\times 10^9} = 2 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nThen $\\Delta x = 2 \\times 10^{-4} \\times 0.04 = 8 \\times 10^{-6}$ m $= 8$ μm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":33,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Since stress is obtained by dividing force by area, stress is a vector quantity.\nReason (R): The force acting on the portion of a body on a specified side of a section has a definite direction.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false. NCERT points out that stress is not a vector, because unlike force it cannot be assigned a specific direction.\nR is true. The force on the part of the body on a chosen side of a section does have a definite direction.\nHaving a directed force on one side does not make stress a vector.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":34,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A crane is designed to lift loads of up to 4 tonnes (1 tonne = 1000 kg). The steel of its rope has a yield strength of $2.5 \\times 10^{8}$ N m$^{-2}$. A safety factor of ten is applied to the load. What is the minimum radius of a rope of circular cross-section? Take g = 10 m/s$^2$.","image":"","options":["2.26 cm","0.71 cm","5.09 cm","1.13 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: M = 4000 kg, safety factor = 10, $\\sigma_y = 2.5 \\times 10^{8}$ N m$^{-2}$.\nFormula: $A \\geq \\frac{10Mg}{\\sigma_y}$ and $r = \\sqrt{\\frac{A}{\\pi}}$.\nSubstituting: $A = \\frac{10 \\times 4000 \\times 10}{2.5 \\times 10^{8}} = 1.6 \\times 10^{-3}$ m$^2$.\nThen $r = \\sqrt{\\frac{1.6 \\times 10^{-3}}{3.14}} \\approx 0.0226$ m.\nSo r ≈ 2.26 cm. (0.71 cm is the radius without the safety factor.)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":35,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If the length of a wire is doubled while its material, cross-sectional area and load are kept the same, its elongation doubles but its Young's modulus does not change.\nReason (R): Young's modulus is a characteristic of the material and does not depend on the dimensions of the sample.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"From $\\Delta L = \\frac{FL}{AY}$, doubling L with F, A and Y fixed doubles $\\Delta L$. So A is true.\nNCERT states that the ratio of stress to strain is a characteristic of the material. So R is true.\nY stays the same precisely because it depends only on the material. The elongation changes only through the length term in the formula, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":36,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A solid aluminium cube of edge 20 cm is subjected to a uniform hydraulic pressure of $3.6 \\times 10^7$ Pa. The bulk modulus of aluminium is 72 GPa. By approximately how much does each edge of the cube shorten? (Use the small-strain approximation.)","image":"","options":["33.3 $\\mu m$","100 $\\mu m$","11.1 $\\mu m$","66.7 $\\mu m$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: p = $3.6\\times10^7$ Pa, B = $72\\times10^9$ Pa, L = 0.20 m.\nVolume strain: ΔV/V = p/B = $5\\times10^{-4}$.\nFor a cube, V = $L^3$, so ΔV/V ≈ 3ΔL/L. This gives ΔL/L = $1.67\\times10^{-4}$.\nSubstituting: ΔL = 0.20 × $1.67\\times10^{-4}$ = $3.33\\times10^{-5}$ m.\nSo each edge shortens by about 33.3 μm. Option (2) wrongly uses ΔL/L = ΔV/V.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":37,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a solid sphere compressed uniformly by a fluid under high pressure, the internal restoring force per unit area is equal in magnitude to the hydraulic pressure.\nStatement II: Under hydraulic stress, the shape of the body changes but its volume remains the same.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The hydraulic stress is equal in magnitude to the applied force per unit area, which is the pressure.\nStatement II is incorrect. Hydraulic compression decreases the volume without any change in geometrical shape. It is the reverse of what the statement says.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":38,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A light wire of cross-sectional area $2\\ mm^2$ hangs vertically from a rigid ceiling. A block of mass 5 kg is attached to its lower end and the system is at rest. What is the tensile stress at any cross-section of the wire? (Take $g = 10\\ m/s^2$.)","image":"","options":["$5.0 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa","$2.5 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa","$2.5 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa","$1.25 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: m = 5 kg, g = 10 $m/s^2$, A = 2 $mm^2$ = $2 \\times 10^{-6}\\ m^2$.\nThe ceiling pulls up with a force equal to the weight, but the tension at any cross-section is just F = mg = 50 N, not 2F.\nFormula: stress = F/A.\nSubstituting: stress = $50/(2 \\times 10^{-6})$.\nSo stress = $2.5 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa. The option $5.0 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa wrongly uses 2F.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":39,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel cable of radius 1.0 cm supports a cable car. For safety, the stress in the cable must not exceed $1.5 \\times 10^{8}$ N m$^{-2}$. What is the maximum load the cable can support?","image":"","options":["11.8 kN","47.1 kN","94.2 kN","15.0 kN"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: r = 1.0 cm = 0.01 m, limiting stress $\\sigma = 1.5 \\times 10^{8}$ N m$^{-2}$.\nFormula: $F_{max} = \\sigma \\times A = \\sigma \\pi r^2$.\nSubstituting: $F_{max} = 1.5 \\times 10^{8} \\times 3.14 \\times (0.01)^2 = 4.71 \\times 10^{4}$ N.\nSo the maximum load is about 47.1 kN.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":40,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Steel is more elastic than rubber, even though rubber stretches far more easily.\nReason (R): A material that stretches less for a given load, i.e. one that needs a larger force to produce the same strain, is considered more elastic.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In daily life we call something that stretches a lot 'elastic', but NCERT calls this a misnomer.\nA material that deforms less under a given load offers a larger restoring stress per unit strain, so it is more elastic.\nSteel needs a very large force for a small strain because its Young's modulus is large. Rubber stretches easily.\nSo A is true, and R is true and correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":41,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation) with List-II (type of stress mainly developed).\nList-I:\n(A) Steel rope of a crane holding a load at rest\n(B) A pillar supporting the roof of a building\n(C) A book pressed by the hand and pushed horizontally while its bottom cover stays fixed\n(D) A solid sphere lying deep inside the ocean\nList-II:\n(I) Shearing stress\n(II) Hydraulic stress\n(III) Compressive stress\n(IV) Tensile stress","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The rope is stretched by equal and opposite forces normal to its cross-section, so it is under tensile stress (IV).\n(B) The pillar is squeezed along its length, so it is under compressive stress (III).\n(C) The forces act parallel to the faces and the top face slides relative to the bottom. This is shearing stress (I).\n(D) The water pushes normally on every point of the sphere's surface, so the stress is hydraulic (II).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":42,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Glass is a brittle material, whereas brass is not.\nReason (R): For a brittle material, the ultimate tensile strength point and the fracture point on its stress-strain curve lie close to each other.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT itself raises the question of why glass is brittle while brass is not.\nR is true. If points D and E are close, the material is brittle. If they are far apart, it is ductile.\nGlass breaks almost immediately after reaching its ultimate strength. Brass undergoes large plastic deformation before fracture.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":43,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A light wire hangs from a rigid ceiling and carries a load of weight 200 N at its lower end. Its cross-sectional area is $2 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$. What is the tensile stress at any cross-section of the wire?","image":"","options":["$2 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa","$5 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa","$1 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa","$4 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: F = 200 N, A = $2 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$.\nThe ceiling pulls up with 200 N and the load pulls down with 200 N. The tension at any section is still F, not 2F.\nFormula: stress = $\\frac{F}{A}$.\nSubstituting: stress = $\\frac{200}{2 \\times 10^{-6}}$.\nSo stress = $1 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa. The value $2 \\times 10^8$ Pa comes from wrongly using 2F.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":44,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A 4 kg mass is tied to the end of a steel wire of unstretched length 1.0 m. It is whirled in a vertical circle, and its angular velocity at the lowest point is 5 rad/s. The wire has cross-sectional area $2.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$ and $Y = 2.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$. Take the radius of the circle as 1.0 m. Find the elongation of the wire when the mass is at the lowest point.","image":"","options":["0.35 mm","0.10 mm","0.25 mm","0.15 mm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $m = 4$ kg, $\\omega = 5$ rad/s, $r = L = 1.0$ m, $A = 2 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$.\nFormula: at the lowest point, $T = mg + m\\omega^2 r$ and $\\Delta L = \\frac{TL}{AY}$.\nSubstituting: $T = 40 + 4 \\times 25 \\times 1 = 140$ N, so $\\Delta L = \\frac{140 \\times 1}{2 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 2 \\times 10^{11}} = 3.5 \\times 10^{-4}$ m.\nSo $\\Delta L = 0.35$ mm.\nUsing only $mg$ gives 0.10 mm, using only $m\\omega^2 r$ gives 0.25 mm, and using the top-point tension gives 0.15 mm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":45,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following materials are described in NCERT as having rather small Young's moduli?\nWood, steel, bone, concrete, glass, copper","image":"","options":["4","3","5","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that metals have large Young's moduli, while wood, bone, concrete and glass have rather small ones.\nWood: small. Bone: small. Concrete: small. Glass: small.\nSteel and copper are metals, so their Young's moduli are large.\nThe count is 4.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":46,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Four identical hollow cylindrical steel columns share the weight of a structure equally. Each column has outer radius $R$ and inner radius $r$.\nAssertion (A): If $r$ is increased while $R$ and the load stay the same, the compressional strain in each column increases.\nReason (R): The strain in each column is $\\frac{Mg}{4\\pi Y (R^2 - r^2)}$, and the annular area $\\pi(R^2 - r^2)$ decreases as $r$ increases.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each column carries $Mg/4$ over the annular area $A = \\pi(R^2 - r^2)$.\nStress is $\\frac{Mg}{4A}$, so strain is $\\frac{Mg}{4\\pi Y(R^2 - r^2)}$. R is true.\nIncreasing $r$ with $R$ fixed makes $R^2 - r^2$ smaller, so the stress and strain both rise. A is true.\nThe smaller load-bearing area is exactly why the strain grows, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":47,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"On the stress-strain curve of a material, the ultimate tensile strength point D and the fracture point E lie very close to each other. Such a material is described as:","image":"","options":["Ductile","Brittle","An elastomer","Ideally plastic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Beyond point D, extra strain occurs even with less force, and the material fractures at E. \nIf D and E are close, the material breaks almost as soon as it reaches its maximum stress, with little plastic flow. Such a material is brittle. \nIf D and E are far apart, the material is ductile, so option (1) describes the opposite case. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":48,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A force of 1100 N produces a strain of 0.1% in a thin copper wire of cross-sectional area 0.1 $cm^2$. What is the Young's modulus of copper given by these data?","image":"","options":["$1.1 \\times 10^{11}$ Pa","$1.1 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa","$1.1 \\times 10^{14}$ Pa","$1.1 \\times 10^{10}$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: F = 1100 N, A = 0.1 $cm^2$ = $10^{-5}$ $m^2$, strain = 0.1% = $10^{-3}$.\nFormula: $Y = \\frac{F/A}{\\varepsilon}$.\nSubstituting: stress = $1100/10^{-5}$ = $1.1 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa, so Y = $1.1 \\times 10^{8}/10^{-3}$.\nSo Y = $1.1 \\times 10^{11}$ Pa. Option (2) is the stress alone, obtained by forgetting to divide by the strain.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":49,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Gases are roughly a million times more compressible than solids.\nReason (R): Molecules in gases are very poorly coupled to their neighbours, whereas atoms in solids are tightly coupled.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The bulk modulus of air is about $10^{-4}$ GPa, while that of solids is about $10^{2}$ GPa. Their compressibilities therefore differ by a factor of about $10^6$.\nR is true. NCERT attributes the incompressibility of solids to tight coupling between neighbouring atoms, and the large compressibility of gases to weak coupling between molecules.\nR therefore explains A directly.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":50,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel wire with $Y = 2 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$ is stretched so that its longitudinal strain is $1 \\times 10^{-3}$, which is within the elastic limit. What is the elastic potential energy stored per unit volume of the wire?","image":"","options":["$2 \\times 10^{5}$ J m$^{-3}$","$1 \\times 10^{5}$ J m$^{-3}$","$5 \\times 10^{4}$ J m$^{-3}$","$1 \\times 10^{8}$ J m$^{-3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $Y = 2 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$, $\\varepsilon = 10^{-3}$.\nFormula: $u = \\frac{1}{2}Y\\varepsilon^2$\nSubstituting: $u = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 10^{11} \\times 10^{-6}$\nSo $u = 1 \\times 10^{5}$ J m$^{-3}$.\nThe value $2 \\times 10^{5}$ results from forgetting the factor $\\frac{1}{2}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":51,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A wire of length L, cross-sectional area A and Young's modulus Y is stretched by l. Here $\\sigma$ is the stress and $\\varepsilon$ is the strain. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(P) Work done in stretching the wire\n(Q) Energy density in terms of $\\sigma$ and $\\varepsilon$\n(R) Energy density in terms of Y and $\\varepsilon$\n(S) Energy density in terms of $\\sigma$ and Y\nList-II\n(1) $\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2Y}$\n(2) $\\frac{1}{2}Y\\varepsilon^2$\n(3) $\\frac{YAl^2}{2L}$\n(4) $\\frac{1}{2}\\sigma\\varepsilon$","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-2, R-4, S-1","P-4, Q-3, R-1, S-2","P-1, Q-4, R-2, S-3","P-3, Q-4, R-2, S-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(P) Integrating $\\frac{YAl}{L}dl$ from 0 to l gives $W = \\frac{YAl^2}{2L}$, so P-3.\n(Q) Divide W by the volume AL: $u = \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma\\varepsilon$, so Q-4.\n(R) Put $\\sigma = Y\\varepsilon$: $u = \\frac{1}{2}Y\\varepsilon^2$, so R-2.\n(S) Put $\\varepsilon = \\sigma/Y$: $u = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2Y}$, so S-1.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":52,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a beam of a given material and span loaded at its centre, increasing its depth reduces the sag much more effectively than increasing its breadth by the same factor.\nReason (R): The sag of such a beam is $\\delta = \\frac{Wl^3}{4bd^3Y}$, so $\\delta \\propto d^{-3}$ but only $\\propto b^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: this is NCERT's sag formula for a centrally loaded beam supported near its ends.\nA is true: doubling the depth cuts the sag to 1/8 of its value, while doubling the breadth only halves it.\nThis difference follows directly from the powers in R, so R explains A.\nA very deep, thin bar can buckle, which is why the I-section is used as a compromise.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":53,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"The bulk modulus is defined as $B = -\\frac{p}{(\\Delta V/V)}$. What does the negative sign in this expression indicate?","image":"","options":["An increase in pressure produces a decrease in volume, so B comes out positive","Bulk modulus is negative for all solids and liquids","Hydraulic stress acts opposite to the normal at each point of the surface","Volume strain always has a negative dimensional formula"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"When pressure increases (p positive), the volume decreases (ΔV negative).\nThe ratio p/(ΔV/V) is therefore negative, and the minus sign makes B positive.\nNCERT notes that for a system in equilibrium, B is always positive.\nOption (3) fails because the fluid's force acts along the normal, perpendicular to the surface. Option (4) fails because strain is dimensionless.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":54,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel wire ($Y = 2.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$) is stretched within its elastic limit so that the tensile stress in it is $2.0 \\times 10^{8}$ N m$^{-2}$. What is the elastic potential energy stored per unit volume of the wire?","image":"","options":["$1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ J m$^{-3}$","$2.0 \\times 10^{5}$ J m$^{-3}$","$5.0 \\times 10^{4}$ J m$^{-3}$","$4.0 \\times 10^{5}$ J m$^{-3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\sigma = 2.0 \\times 10^{8}$ N m$^{-2}$ and $Y = 2.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$.\nFormula: $u = \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma\\varepsilon$. Since $\\varepsilon = \\frac{\\sigma}{Y}$, this becomes $u = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2Y}$.\nSubstituting: $u = \\frac{(2.0 \\times 10^{8})^2}{2 \\times 2.0 \\times 10^{11}} = \\frac{4 \\times 10^{16}}{4 \\times 10^{11}}$.\nSo $u = 1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ J m$^{-3}$. Dropping the factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ would wrongly give $2.0 \\times 10^{5}$ J m$^{-3}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":55,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Steel is more elastic than rubber.\nStatement II: For the same applied stress, a more elastic material undergoes a larger strain.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. NCERT says a material that stretches less for a given load is more elastic. Steel stretches far less than rubber, so steel is more elastic.\nStatement II: Incorrect. A more elastic material has a larger modulus ($Y = \\frac{stress}{strain}$). For the same stress it therefore shows a smaller strain, not a larger one.\nThe everyday idea that 'stretches more means more elastic' is a misnomer.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":56,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"The shear moduli of some materials (in GPa) are: aluminium 25, brass 36, copper 42, iron 70, lead 5.6, nickel 77, steel 84, tungsten 150.\nHow many of these materials have a shear modulus greater than 50 GPa?","image":"","options":["5","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Compare each value with 50 GPa.\nGreater than 50 GPa: iron (70), nickel (77), steel (84) and tungsten (150). That makes 4.\nLess than 50 GPa: aluminium (25), brass (36), copper (42) and lead (5.6).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":57,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A rectangular block has Young's modulus $Y = 1.5 \\times 10^{11}$ Pa. Assume its shear modulus is $G = Y/3$. The block is 5.0 cm high, and its top face measures 10 cm × 4.0 cm. The bottom face is fixed. What tangential force on the top face will displace it sideways by 0.010 mm?","image":"","options":["$1.2 \\times 10^{5}$ N","$1.0 \\times 10^{4}$ N","$4.0 \\times 10^{4}$ N","$8.0 \\times 10^{3}$ N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $G = \\frac{1.5 \\times 10^{11}}{3} = 5.0 \\times 10^{10}$ Pa, $\\Delta x = 1.0 \\times 10^{-5}$ m, $L = 0.05$ m, $A = 0.10 \\times 0.04 = 4.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m².\nStrain: $\\frac{\\Delta x}{L} = 2.0 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nStress: $G \\times \\text{strain} = 5.0 \\times 10^{10} \\times 2.0 \\times 10^{-4} = 1.0 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa.\nForce: $F = \\text{stress} \\times A = 1.0 \\times 10^{7} \\times 4.0 \\times 10^{-3} = 4.0 \\times 10^{4}$ N.\nUsing Y in place of G would wrongly give $1.2 \\times 10^{5}$ N.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":58,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A copper wire and a steel wire are joined end to end. Each wire is 1.0 m long and has a cross-sectional area of $2.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$. The combination hangs vertically and supports a load of 22 kg at its lower end. Neglect the masses of the wires. Take $Y_{copper} = 1.1 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$, $Y_{steel} = 2.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$ and $g = 10$ m s$^{-2}$. The total elastic potential energy stored in the two wires is approximately:","image":"","options":["0.11 J","0.061 J","0.17 J","0.34 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: the tension is the same in both wires, $F = 22 \\times 10 = 220$ N; $A = 2.0\\times10^{-6}$ m$^2$; $L = 1.0$ m for each.\nFormula: $U = \\frac{1}{2}F\\Delta L = \\frac{F^2L}{2AY}$ for each wire.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{F^2}{2A} = \\frac{48400}{4\\times10^{-6}} = 1.21\\times10^{10}$.\nCopper: $1.21\\times10^{10}\\times\\frac{1}{1.1\\times10^{11}} = 0.110$ J.\nSteel: $1.21\\times10^{10}\\times\\frac{1}{2.0\\times10^{11}} = 0.0605$ J.\nSo total $U \\approx 0.17$ J.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":59,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Engineers design a crane with a lifting capacity of 5 metric tons (1 metric ton = 1000 kg). The load must never deform the mild-steel rope permanently. A safety factor of 10 on the load is used. Yield strength of mild steel = $3 \\times 10^{8}$ N m$^{-2}$; take g = 10 m/s$^2$. What is the minimum radius of a rope of circular cross-section?","image":"","options":["0.73 cm","2.3 cm","4.6 cm","7.3 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: M = 5000 kg, safety factor = 10, $\\sigma_y = 3 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa.\nFormula: to avoid permanent set, stress must stay below the yield strength, so $A \\geq \\frac{10Mg}{\\sigma_y}$.\nSubstituting: $A = \\frac{10 \\times 5000 \\times 10}{3 \\times 10^{8}} = 1.67 \\times 10^{-3}$ m$^2$. Then $r = \\sqrt{A/\\pi} \\approx 0.023$ m.\nSo r ≈ 2.3 cm. Ignoring the safety factor gives 0.73 cm. Quoting the diameter instead gives 4.6 cm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":60,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Young's modulus is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Its SI unit is N $m^{-2}$, the same as that of stress","Its dimensional formula is $[MLT^{-2}]$ because strain is dimensionless","It is the ratio of tensile (or compressive) stress to longitudinal strain","It can be written as $Y = \\frac{FL}{A\\Delta L}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: strain has no unit, so Y has the same unit as stress, N $m^{-2}$ or Pa.\n(2) Incorrect: Y has the dimensions of stress, $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$. $[MLT^{-2}]$ is the dimension of force.\n(3) Correct: this is the definition, $Y = \\sigma/\\varepsilon$.\n(4) Correct: $Y = \\frac{F/A}{\\Delta L/L} = \\frac{FL}{A\\Delta L}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":61,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Two wires P and Q have identical length and cross-sectional area. They carry the same load. Wire P stretches by 2 mm and wire Q stretches by 0.5 mm. Which statement is correct?","image":"","options":["Q is more elastic, because its Young's modulus is larger","P is more elastic, because it stretches more","Both are equally elastic, because they carry the same load","P is more elastic, because its Young's modulus is larger"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: NCERT points out that calling a material that stretches more 'more elastic' is a misnomer. The material that stretches less for a given load is the more elastic one.\nWith F, L and A the same, $Y = \\frac{FL}{A\\Delta L}$, so Y is inversely proportional to $\\Delta L$.\nQ stretches less, so Q has the larger Y and is more elastic. Option (2) uses the everyday meaning of 'elastic', which is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":62,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When a solid sphere is placed in a fluid under high pressure, its volume decreases.\nStatement II: Under such hydraulic compression, the geometrical shape of the sphere also changes.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. The fluid presses perpendicular to the surface at every point, so the sphere is compressed uniformly and its volume decreases.\nStatement II is false. Hydraulic compression produces a volume strain $\\Delta V/V$ without any change in geometrical shape.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":63,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A wire is gradually stretched within its elastic limit. When the stretching force reaches 100 N, the wire has extended by 2 mm. How much elastic potential energy is stored in the wire?","image":"","options":["0.05 J","100 J","0.2 J","0.1 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $F = 100$ N, $l = 2$ mm $= 2 \\times 10^{-3}$ m.\nFormula: $U = \\frac{1}{2}Fl$, because the force rises linearly from zero.\nSubstituting: $U = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 100 \\times 2 \\times 10^{-3}$\nSo $U = 0.1$ J.\nThe option 100 J comes from leaving the extension in mm.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":64,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A metal wire is loaded beyond its yield point to some point C between B and D on its stress-strain curve, and the load is then removed. The wire does not return to its original length.\nReason (R): Beyond the yield point the material acquires a permanent set, so the strain is not zero even when the stress becomes zero.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Once the stress exceeds the yield strength (point B), unloading from a point C between B and D does not restore the original length. \nR is true. At zero stress the strain remains non-zero; this is called permanent set, and the deformation is plastic. \nThe permanent set is exactly why the wire stays longer, so R correctly explains A. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":65,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Two wires of the same material and the same length, but of different radii, are joined end to end and loaded. The thinner wire elongates more than the thicker one.\nReason (R): For the same tension, length and Young's modulus, the elongation of a wire is inversely proportional to its area of cross-section.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In a series joint, both wires carry the same tension F.\nFrom $\\Delta L = \\frac{FL}{AY}$, if F, L and Y are equal, then $\\Delta L \\propto \\frac{1}{A}$. So R is true.\nThe thinner wire has the smaller A, so it elongates more. A is true.\nR directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":66,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Two wires A and B are made of the same material. Wire A has length L and diameter d. Wire B has length 2L and diameter 2d. Each wire is stretched by the same load, within the elastic limit.\nStatement I: The elongation of A is twice the elongation of B.\nStatement II: The elastic potential energy stored in B is twice that stored in A.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Elongation: $\\Delta L = \\frac{FL}{AY}$, so $\\Delta L \\propto \\frac{L}{d^2}$. For A this gives $\\frac{L}{d^2}$; for B, $\\frac{2L}{4d^2} = \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{L}{d^2}$. So A stretches twice as much as B, and Statement I is correct.\nEnergy: $U = \\frac{1}{2}F\\Delta L$. With the same F, $U \\propto \\Delta L$, so $U_A = 2U_B$.\nStatement II reverses this, so it is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":67,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"An upper wire of cross-sectional area 2 mm$^2$ hangs from a ceiling and carries a block P of mass 6 kg. A lower wire of cross-sectional area 1 mm$^2$ hangs from P and carries a block Q of mass 4 kg. Both wires are light. Take g = 10 m/s$^2$. What is the ratio of the tensile stress in the upper wire to that in the lower wire?","image":"","options":["0.75","2.5","0.625","1.25"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: the upper wire supports P and Q, so its tension is (6 + 4) $\\times$ 10 = 100 N. The lower wire supports only Q, so its tension is 4 $\\times$ 10 = 40 N.\nFormula: stress = $\\frac{T}{A}$.\nSubstituting: upper stress = $\\frac{100}{2 \\times 10^{-6}} = 5 \\times 10^7$ Pa. Lower stress = $\\frac{40}{1 \\times 10^{-6}} = 4 \\times 10^7$ Pa.\nSo the ratio = $\\frac{5 \\times 10^7}{4 \\times 10^7}$ = 1.25. Forgetting block Q in the upper wire would give 0.75.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":68,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"What is the dimensional formula of strain?","image":"","options":["$[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$","$[M^0L^0T^0]$","$[L]$","$[MLT^{-2}]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Strain is the ratio of a change in dimension to the original dimension, such as $\\Delta L/L$, $\\Delta x/L$ or $\\Delta V/V$.\nA ratio of two like quantities has no unit and no dimensions, so its formula is $[M^0L^0T^0]$.\nThe tempting option $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$ is the dimensional formula of stress, not strain.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":69,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(P) Shearing strain\n(Q) SI unit of shear modulus\n(R) Shearing stress\n(S) Shear modulus of most materials (approximately)\nList-II:\n(1) $G\\theta$\n(2) $\\frac{Y}{3}$\n(3) $\\tan\\theta \\approx \\theta$\n(4) N m$^{-2}$","image":"","options":["P-1, Q-4, R-3, S-2","P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2","P-3, Q-2, R-1, S-4","P-4, Q-3, R-2, S-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(P) Shearing strain is $\\frac{\\Delta x}{L} = \\tan\\theta \\approx \\theta$, so P-3.\n(Q) G is stress divided by a dimensionless strain, so its unit is N m$^{-2}$ (Pa): Q-4.\n(R) From the definition of G, $\\sigma_s = G\\theta$: R-1.\n(S) For most materials $G \\approx Y/3$: S-2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":70,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Strain has no unit.\n(ii) Shearing strain changes the shape of a body but not its volume.\n(iii) Volume strain due to hydraulic stress involves no change in shape.\n(iv) For a given material, a tensile stress and a compressive stress of equal magnitude produce equal magnitudes of longitudinal strain.\n(v) Shearing strain is the displacement of the upper face divided by the area of that face.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: strain is a ratio of like quantities, so it has no unit.\n(ii) Correct: NCERT's table lists shear as producing a change in shape but no change in volume.\n(iii) Correct: hydraulic compression changes only the volume.\n(iv) Correct: experiments show the strain magnitude is the same for tensile and compressive stress.\n(v) Incorrect: shearing strain is $\\Delta x/L$, where $L$ is a length, not an area.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":71,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A copper wire and a steel wire of different diameters are joined end to end. The combination hangs vertically from a rigid support and a load is attached at the lower end. Neglect the masses of the wires. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["The tension is greater in the thinner wire, so the stress is also greater in it","The stress is the same in both wires because they carry the same load","The tension is the same in both wires, but the stress is greater in the thinner wire","The longitudinal strain is the same in both wires because the tension is the same"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Each wire must transmit the full load. So both wires carry the same tension, equal to the weight W.\nStress = W/A. The thinner wire has the smaller area, so it has the larger stress.\nStrain = stress/Y. Both the stress and Y differ between the wires, so the strains are not equal in general.\nOption (2) fails because equal tension does not mean equal stress when the areas differ.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":72,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Stress-strain curves of two materials P and Q are drawn to the same scale (stress on the vertical axis, strain on the horizontal axis). P's initial straight portion is steeper than Q's. P reaches its maximum stress and breaks almost immediately. Q reaches a lower maximum stress but then stretches over a long portion of the curve before it breaks. Which conclusion is correct?","image":"","options":["P has the larger Young's modulus and is brittle; Q is ductile","P has the smaller Young's modulus because it fractures at a smaller strain","Q is brittle because it fractures at a stress lower than its ultimate tensile strength","Q is the stronger material because it breaks at a larger strain"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Young's modulus is the slope of the linear part (stress/strain). P's steeper line means P has the larger Y, so (2) is wrong. \nFor P, the ultimate point and the fracture point nearly coincide, so P is brittle. \nFor Q, they are far apart, so Q is ductile. Option (3) has this reversed. \nStrength depends on the maximum stress the material can bear, not the strain at breaking. P bears more stress, so (4) is wrong. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":73,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform rigid bar hangs horizontally from three vertical wires of equal length. The two end wires are copper and the middle wire is iron. Each wire carries the same tension, and the bar stays horizontal. Take $Y_{Cu} = 1.1 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$ and $Y_{Fe} = 1.9 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$. Find the ratio of the diameter of a copper wire to that of the iron wire.","image":"","options":["1.31","0.76","1.73","0.58"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: equal tension $F$, equal length $L$, and equal extension $\\Delta L$ (the bar stays level).\nFormula: $\\Delta L = \\frac{FL}{AY}$, so equal $F$, $L$ and $\\Delta L$ require $AY$ to be the same for each wire.\nSince $A \\propto d^2$, we get $d^2 Y = $ constant, so $\\frac{d_{Cu}}{d_{Fe}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{Y_{Fe}}{Y_{Cu}}}$.\nSubstituting: $\\sqrt{1.9/1.1} = \\sqrt{1.727}$.\nSo the ratio is $\\approx 1.31$. The value 1.73 forgets the square root.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":74,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Between the proportional limit and the yield point, stress is not proportional to strain, yet the body regains its original dimensions on unloading.\n(ii) A material whose ultimate strength point and fracture point are close together is brittle.\n(iii) Elastomers such as the elastic tissue of the aorta obey Hooke's law over most of their elastic region.\n(iv) Beyond the ultimate tensile strength point, additional strain is produced even by a reduced applied force.\n(v) If the load is removed at a point between the yield point and the ultimate strength point, the strain returns to zero when the stress becomes zero.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: in region AB the curve is non-linear but the deformation is still elastic.\n(ii) Correct: when D and E are close the material is brittle; when they are far apart it is ductile.\n(iii) Incorrect: the aorta tissue has a large elastic region but does not obey Hooke's law over most of it.\n(iv) Correct: this is how the curve behaves between D and E.\n(v) Incorrect: the body keeps a permanent set, so the strain is not zero at zero stress.\nCorrect statements = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":75,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A beam supported near its ends and loaded at the centre sags by $\\delta = \\frac{Wl^3}{4bd^3Y}$. The load, length, breadth and material stay the same, but the depth d of the beam is doubled. The sag becomes:","image":"","options":["half","one-fourth","one-eighth","double"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"From the formula, $\\delta \\propto d^{-3}$ when W, l, b and Y are fixed.\nIf d becomes 2d, the sag is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{2^3} = \\frac{1}{8}$.\n'Half' would be the result of doubling the breadth, because $\\delta \\propto b^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":76,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"The steel ropes of cranes are made of many thin wires braided together instead of a single thick wire of the same radius. According to NCERT, what is the main reason?","image":"","options":["A single thick wire would have a much lower Young's modulus","Braiding gives ease of manufacture, flexibility and strength, while a single thick wire would be practically a rigid rod","Thin wires have a higher yield strength only because of their larger surface area","Braiding removes the need for any margin of safety in the load"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: a single wire of radius about 3 cm would practically behave as a rigid rod.\nSo ropes are made of thin wires braided together, for ease of manufacture, flexibility and strength.\nOption (1) is wrong because Young's modulus is a property of the material, not of the thickness.\nOption (4) is wrong because a safety factor of about ten is still provided.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":77,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel rod and an aluminium rod carry equal tensile loads and must undergo equal elongations. The aluminium rod is 1.5 times as long as the steel rod. Take $Y_{steel} = 2.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N $m^{-2}$ and $Y_{Al} = 7.0 \\times 10^{10}$ N $m^{-2}$. What is the ratio of the radius of the aluminium rod to that of the steel rod, $r_{Al}/r_{s}$?","image":"","options":["2.07","1.69","4.29","1.38"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: same F, same $\\Delta L$, and $L_{Al} = 1.5L_s$.\nFormula: $\\Delta L = \\frac{FL}{\\pi r^2 Y}$. Equal $\\Delta L$ requires $\\frac{L}{r^2 Y}$ to be equal for both rods.\nSo $\\frac{r_{Al}^2}{r_s^2} = \\frac{Y_s}{Y_{Al}} \\times \\frac{L_{Al}}{L_s} = \\frac{2.0}{0.70} \\times 1.5 = 4.29$.\nSo $r_{Al}/r_s = \\sqrt{4.29}$ ≈ 2.07.\nOption (2) ignores the length ratio. Option (3) forgets to take the square root.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":78,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A copper wire of length 1.0 m and a steel wire of length 2.0 m, each of diameter 2.0 mm, are joined end to end. When the combination is stretched by a load, the total elongation is 0.50 mm. Find the load. ($Y_{Cu} = 1.1 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$, $Y_{steel} = 2.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["157 N","82.3 N","41.1 N","329 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: the tension F is the same in both wires. Radius = 1.0 mm, so $A = \\pi (10^{-3})^2 = 3.14 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$.\nFormula: total elongation $= \\frac{F}{A}\\left(\\frac{L_c}{Y_c} + \\frac{L_s}{Y_s}\\right)$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1.0}{1.1 \\times 10^{11}} + \\frac{2.0}{2.0 \\times 10^{11}} = 1.909 \\times 10^{-11}$ m$^3$ N$^{-1}$, so $F = \\frac{5 \\times 10^{-4} \\times 3.14 \\times 10^{-6}}{1.909 \\times 10^{-11}}$.\nSo F ≈ 82.3 N.\nTaking the diameter as the radius gives 329 N, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":79,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (material) with List-II (bulk modulus, as given in NCERT):\nList-I\n(A) Copper\n(B) Glass\n(C) Water\n(D) Air (at STP)\nList-II\n(I) 2.2 GPa\n(II) $1.0\\times10^{-4}$ GPa\n(III) 140 GPa\n(IV) 37 GPa","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Copper is a metal solid with B = 140 GPa, so A-III.\nGlass is a solid but less stiff, with B = 37 GPa, so B-IV.\nWater is a liquid, with B = 2.2 GPa, so C-I.\nAir is a gas, with B = $1.0\\times10^{-4}$ GPa, so D-II.\nThe values follow the order solids > liquids > gases.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":80,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"On a hypothetical planet, the acceleration due to gravity is 4 m s$^{-2}$. The rocks there have a density of $3 \\times 10^3$ kg m$^{-3}$, and the critical (elastic-limit) shearing stress at which they begin to flow is $30 \\times 10^7$ N m$^{-2}$. Use the same reasoning NCERT applies to mountains on Earth. What is the maximum possible height of a mountain on this planet?","image":"","options":["10 km","25 km","4 km","62.5 km"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $g = 4$ m s$^{-2}$, $\\rho = 3\\times10^3$ kg m$^{-3}$, critical stress $= 3\\times10^8$ N m$^{-2}$.\nThe base is not under uniform (bulk) compression, because the sides of the mountain are free. The shear component there is about $h\\rho g$.\nCondition: $h\\rho g \\le 3\\times10^8$ N m$^{-2}$.\nSubstituting: $h = \\frac{3\\times10^8}{3\\times10^3\\times4} = 2.5\\times10^4$ m.\nSo $h_{max} = 25$ km. A weaker g allows taller mountains; 10 km is the Earth value.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":81,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"An aluminium cube of edge 20 cm is placed in a chamber in which the hydraulic pressure is increased by $3.6 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa. The bulk modulus of aluminium is 72 GPa. What is the decrease in the volume of the cube?","image":"","options":["4.0 $cm^3$","0.5 $cm^3$","2.0 $cm^3$","8.0 $cm^3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $V = (0.2)^3 = 8 \\times 10^{-3}$ $m^3$, $p = 3.6 \\times 10^7$ Pa, $B = 72 \\times 10^9$ Pa.\nFormula: $|\\Delta V| = \\frac{pV}{B}$.\nSubstituting: $|\\Delta V| = \\frac{3.6 \\times 10^7 \\times 8 \\times 10^{-3}}{72 \\times 10^9} = 4 \\times 10^{-6}$ $m^3$.\nSince 1 $m^3$ = $10^6$ $cm^3$, the volume decreases by 4.0 $cm^3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":82,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A solid steel ball is lowered from the surface of the sea to a depth of 4000 m. The density of sea water is 1000 kg $m^{-3}$ and the bulk modulus of steel is $1.6 \\times 10^{11}$ N $m^{-2}$. Consider only the extra pressure due to the water column and take g = 10 m/s^2. The fractional decrease in the radius of the ball, $\\frac{\\Delta r}{r}$, is closest to:","image":"","options":["$8.3 \\times 10^{-5}$","$25 \\times 10^{-5}$","$2.8 \\times 10^{-5}$","$75 \\times 10^{-5}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: h = 4000 m, $\\rho$ = 1000 kg $m^{-3}$, g = 10 m/s^2, $B = 1.6 \\times 10^{11}$ Pa.\nExtra pressure: $p = h\\rho g = 4 \\times 10^7$ Pa.\nVolume strain: $\\frac{\\Delta V}{V} = \\frac{p}{B} = \\frac{4 \\times 10^7}{1.6 \\times 10^{11}} = 2.5 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nFor a sphere, $V \\propto r^3$, so $\\frac{\\Delta V}{V} = 3\\frac{\\Delta r}{r}$.\nSo $\\frac{\\Delta r}{r} = \\frac{2.5 \\times 10^{-4}}{3} \\approx 8.3 \\times 10^{-5}$.\nThe tempting option (2) is the volume strain itself, not the radius strain.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":83,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"The property of a body by which it tends to regain its original size and shape after the applied force is removed is called:","image":"","options":["Plasticity","Elasticity","Ductility","Rigidity"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines elasticity as the tendency of a body to regain its original size and shape once the deforming force is removed.\nThe deformation it undergoes is called elastic deformation, as when a gently stretched helical spring returns to its length.\nPlasticity is the opposite behaviour, where the body stays permanently deformed.\nRigidity describes an idealised body that does not deform at all, so it is not the property of recovering shape.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":84,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Young's modulus is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["For a given material, the strain produced by a compressive stress differs in magnitude from the strain produced by a tensile stress of equal magnitude","Young's modulus is a characteristic of the material and does not depend on the length of the wire","Young's modulus can be written as $\\frac{FL}{A\\Delta L}$","Metals generally have large Young's moduli, so a large force produces only a small change in their length"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: experiments show that a given material gives the same magnitude of strain whether the stress is tensile or compressive.\n(2) Correct: the ratio of stress to strain is a characteristic of the material.\n(3) Correct: $Y = \\frac{F/A}{\\Delta L/L} = \\frac{FL}{A\\Delta L}$.\n(4) Correct: this follows directly from the data in Table 8.1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":85,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel cube has one face firmly fixed to a vertical wall. A mass of 840 kg is hung from the opposite face, so its weight acts as a vertical shearing force. The shear angle must not exceed $1.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ rad. Take G(steel) = 84 GPa and g = 10 m/s$^2$. If the cube has the smallest edge that satisfies this condition, what is the vertical deflection of the loaded face?","image":"","options":["$3.16 \\times 10^{-7}$ m","$1.0 \\times 10^{-7}$ m","$1.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m","$3.16 \\times 10^{-6}$ m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: F = Mg = 8400 N, G = $84 \\times 10^{9}$ Pa, $\\theta_{max} = 10^{-6}$ rad.\nFormula: $\\theta = \\frac{F}{AG} = \\frac{F}{L^2G}$, so $L_{min}^2 = \\frac{F}{G\\theta_{max}}$.\nSubstituting: $L_{min}^2 = \\frac{8400}{84 \\times 10^{9} \\times 10^{-6}} = 0.1$ m$^2$, giving $L_{min} = 0.316$ m.\nDeflection: $\\Delta x = \\theta L = 10^{-6} \\times 0.316 = 3.16 \\times 10^{-7}$ m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":86,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A force of 1100 N is needed to increase the length of a thin copper wire of cross-sectional area 0.1 cm$^2$ by 0.1%. What is the Young's modulus of copper according to these data?","image":"","options":["$1.1 \\times 10^{11}$ Pa","$1.1 \\times 10^{10}$ Pa","$1.1 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa","$1.1 \\times 10^{9}$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: F = 1100 N, A = 0.1 cm$^2$ = $10^{-5}$ m$^2$, strain = 0.1% = $10^{-3}$.\nFormula: $Y = \\frac{F/A}{\\text{strain}}$.\nSubstituting: stress = $\\frac{1100}{10^{-5}} = 1.1 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa, so $Y = \\frac{1.1 \\times 10^{8}}{10^{-3}}$.\nSo $Y = 1.1 \\times 10^{11}$ Pa. Option (3) is the stress alone; it forgets to divide by the strain.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":87,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Longitudinal strain\n(B) Shearing strain\n(C) Volume strain\n(D) Lateral strain in a stretched wire\nList-II:\n(i) $\\Delta V/V$\n(ii) $\\Delta d/d$\n(iii) $\\Delta L/L$\n(iv) $\\Delta x/L$","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-ii, C-i, D-iv","A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) Longitudinal strain is the change in length over the original length, $\\Delta L/L$, so A matches (iii).\n(B) Shearing strain is the relative displacement of the faces over the length, $\\Delta x/L$, so B matches (iv).\n(C) Volume strain is $\\Delta V/V$, so C matches (i).\n(D) Lateral strain is the change in diameter over the original diameter, $\\Delta d/d$, so D matches (ii).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":88,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel wire of length 1.5 m hangs from the ceiling, and a 4.0 kg block is attached to its lower end. A brass wire of length 1.0 m hangs from this block and carries a 6.0 kg block at its lower end. Both wires have the same diameter. Take $Y_{steel} = 2.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$ and $Y_{brass} = 0.90 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$, and neglect the masses of the wires. What is the ratio of the elongation of the steel wire to that of the brass wire? (g = 10 m s$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["0.675","0.75","2.5","1.125"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Tensions: the steel wire supports both blocks, so $T_s$ = (4 + 6) × 10 = 100 N. The brass wire supports only the lower block, so $T_b$ = 6 × 10 = 60 N.\nFormula: $\\Delta L = \\frac{TL}{AY}$, and A is the same for both wires.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{\\Delta L_s}{\\Delta L_b} = \\frac{100 \\times 1.5/2.0}{60 \\times 1.0/0.90} = \\frac{75}{66.7}$.\nSo the ratio is 1.125.\nOption (1) wrongly takes 60 N as the tension in the steel wire.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":89,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The response of a stretched wire cannot be described completely by its Young's modulus alone.\nReason (R): A force applied along the length of a wire also changes its lateral dimensions, and this change is described by a separate elastic constant called Poisson's ratio.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that a force in one direction can produce strains in other directions too, so one elastic constant is not enough.\nR is true. The radius of a stretched wire decreases, and Poisson's ratio describes this lateral change.\nR directly explains why Young's modulus alone is insufficient.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":90,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel wire with Poisson's ratio 0.30 has length 3.0 m and diameter 2.0 mm. A load stretches it by 1.5 mm within the elastic limit. By how much does its diameter decrease?","image":"","options":["0.60 $\\mu m$","0.30 $\\mu m$","0.15 $\\mu m$","3.0 $\\mu m$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $L = 3.0$ m, $\\Delta L = 1.5 \\times 10^{-3}$ m, $d = 2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m, Poisson's ratio = 0.30.\nLongitudinal strain: $\\frac{1.5 \\times 10^{-3}}{3.0} = 5.0 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nLateral strain: $0.30 \\times 5.0 \\times 10^{-4} = 1.5 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nDecrease in diameter: $\\Delta d = 1.5 \\times 10^{-4} \\times 2.0 \\times 10^{-3} = 3.0 \\times 10^{-7}$ m.\nSo $\\Delta d$ = 0.30 $\\mu m$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":91,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If a steel wire is replaced by another steel wire of the same material but twice the length, the Poisson's ratio of the wire doubles.\nStatement II: Poisson's ratio depends only on the nature of the material.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement II is true. NCERT says the value of Poisson's ratio depends only on the nature of the material.\nStatement I is false. Changing the length changes the elongation, but both strains scale together, so their ratio stays the same for the same steel.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":92,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Thin wires of four metals, each of cross-sectional area 0.1 $cm^2$, are to be stretched by 0.1%. Match each metal in List-I with the force required in List-II, as given in NCERT.\nList-I: (A) Steel (B) Copper (C) Brass (D) Aluminium\nList-II: (P) 690 N (Q) 900 N (R) 1100 N (S) 2000 N","image":"","options":["A-S, B-R, C-Q, D-P","A-S, B-Q, C-R, D-P","A-R, B-S, C-Q, D-P","A-S, B-R, C-P, D-Q"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For the same area and the same strain, the force needed is proportional to Y.\nSteel has the largest Y, so it needs 2000 N (A-S).\nCopper needs 1100 N (B-R).\nBrass needs 900 N (C-Q).\nAluminium has the smallest Y of the four, so it needs 690 N (D-P).\nThe order of Y is therefore steel > copper > brass > aluminium.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":93,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"For a wire of Young's modulus $Y$ stretched within its proportional limit (stress $\\sigma$, strain $\\varepsilon$), how many of the following expressions/statements about the elastic potential energy per unit volume $u$ are correct?\n(i) $u = \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma\\varepsilon$\n(ii) $u = \\frac{1}{2}Y\\varepsilon^2$\n(iii) $u = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2Y}$\n(iv) $u$ has the same dimensions as stress\n(v) $u = \\frac{1}{2}Y\\sigma^2$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the NCERT result $u = \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma\\varepsilon$.\n(ii) Correct: substituting $\\sigma = Y\\varepsilon$ gives $\\frac{1}{2}Y\\varepsilon^2$.\n(iii) Correct: substituting $\\varepsilon = \\sigma/Y$ gives $\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2Y}$.\n(iv) Correct: J m$^{-3}$ = N m$^{-2}$, which is $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$.\n(v) Incorrect: this expression is dimensionally wrong.\nSo 4 are correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":94,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"The maximum height of a mountain is limited because the shear stress at its base (approximately $h\\rho g$) must not exceed the elastic limit of the rock. On a planet where $g = 5$ m/s$^2$, the rock has an elastic limit of $3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ N m$^{-2}$ and density $2.5 \\times 10^{3}$ kg m$^{-3}$. The maximum possible height of a mountain on this planet is about:","image":"","options":["10 km","24 km","12 km","48 km"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: elastic limit = $3.0\\times10^8$ N m$^{-2}$, $\\rho = 2.5\\times10^3$ kg m$^{-3}$, $g = 5$ m s$^{-2}$.\nFormula: $h\\rho g = $ elastic limit, so $h = \\frac{\\text{elastic limit}}{\\rho g}$.\nSubstituting: $h = \\frac{3.0\\times10^8}{2.5\\times10^3\\times5} = 2.4\\times10^4$ m.\nSo $h \\approx 24$ km. Note that 12 km is the result if $g = 10$ is wrongly used.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":95,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel wire of radius 1.0 mm is stretched by a force of 314 N applied along its length. What is the tensile stress in the wire? (Take $\\pi = 3.14$)","image":"","options":["$1.0 \\times 10^{8}$ N $m^{-2}$","$1.0 \\times 10^{2}$ N $m^{-2}$","$2.5 \\times 10^{7}$ N $m^{-2}$","$3.14 \\times 10^{8}$ N $m^{-2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: F = 314 N, r = 1.0 mm = $10^{-3}$ m.\nFormula: stress = $\\frac{F}{\\pi r^2}$.\nSubstituting: area = $3.14 \\times 10^{-6}$ $m^2$, so stress = $314/(3.14 \\times 10^{-6})$.\nSo stress = $1.0 \\times 10^{8}$ N $m^{-2}$.\nOption (2) comes from leaving the radius in mm. Option (3) comes from using the diameter in place of the radius.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":96,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Two wires P and Q are made of the same material and have the same length. Wire P has radius $r$ and wire Q has radius $2r$. Each wire is stretched by the same load $F$, within the elastic limit.\nStatement I: The elastic potential energy stored in P is four times that stored in Q.\nStatement II: For a given load, the elastic potential energy stored per unit volume of a wire does not depend on its cross-sectional area.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The elongation is $l = \\frac{FL}{AY}$, so the stored energy is $U = \\frac{1}{2}Fl = \\frac{F^2L}{2AY}$, which is proportional to $\\frac{1}{A}$.\nStatement I: Correct. $A_P : A_Q = r^2 : 4r^2 = 1 : 4$, so $U_P : U_Q = 4 : 1$.\nStatement II: Incorrect. Energy density is $u = \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2Y}$ with $\\sigma = \\frac{F}{A}$. So $u \\propto \\frac{1}{A^2}$ and does depend on the area. P stores 16 times the energy density of Q.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":97,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform rigid bar is hung horizontally by three vertical wires of equal length. The wires are attached at the two ends and at the middle of the bar. The two end wires are made of aluminium ($Y = 7.0 \\times 10^{10}$ N m$^{-2}$) and the middle wire is made of steel ($Y = 2.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$). Each wire is to carry the same tension. What is the ratio of the diameter of an aluminium wire to that of the steel wire?","image":"","options":["0.59","1.69","2.86","0.35"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The bar is rigid and stays horizontal, so all three wires stretch by the same amount $\\Delta L$.\nFormula: $\\Delta L = \\frac{TL}{AY}$. With T, L and $\\Delta L$ equal for all wires, $AY$ must be the same, so $d^2 \\propto \\frac{1}{Y}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{d_{Al}}{d_s} = \\sqrt{\\frac{Y_s}{Y_{Al}}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2.0}{0.70}} = 1.69$.\nOption (3) is the ratio of the Young's moduli, without the square root.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":98,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation) with List-II (type of strain produced).\nList-I:\n(A) A book on a table pushed horizontally by a hand pressed on its top cover\n(B) A solid sphere placed in a fluid under high pressure\n(C) A wire hanging from a ceiling with a load at its lower end\n(D) Thighbones of the bottom performer in a circus human pyramid\nList-II:\n(i) Compressive longitudinal strain\n(ii) Shearing strain\n(iii) Volume strain without change of shape\n(iv) Tensile longitudinal strain","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) A tangential force on the top face makes the faces slide relative to each other: shearing strain, so (ii).\n(B) The fluid presses normally and uniformly at every point: volume strain with no shape change, so (iii).\n(C) The load stretches the wire along its length: tensile strain, so (iv).\n(D) The weight of 220 kg pushes the femurs along their length and shortens them: compressive strain, so (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":99,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (elastic quantity) with List-II (defining expression).\nList-I:\n(A) Young's modulus\n(B) Shear modulus\n(C) Bulk modulus\n(D) Compressibility\nList-II:\n(P) $-\\frac{p}{\\Delta V/V}$\n(Q) $\\frac{F}{A\\theta}$\n(R) $\\frac{FL}{A\\Delta L}$\n(S) $-\\frac{1}{\\Delta p}\\frac{\\Delta V}{V}$","image":"","options":["A-Q, B-R, C-P, D-S","A-R, B-Q, C-P, D-S","A-R, B-Q, C-S, D-P","A-P, B-Q, C-R, D-S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) Young's modulus = $\\frac{F/A}{\\Delta L/L} = \\frac{FL}{A\\Delta L}$, so A-R.\n(B) Shear modulus = $\\frac{F/A}{\\theta} = \\frac{F}{A\\theta}$, so B-Q.\n(C) Bulk modulus = $-\\frac{p}{\\Delta V/V}$, so C-P.\n(D) Compressibility is the reciprocal of B, $k = -\\frac{1}{\\Delta p}\\frac{\\Delta V}{V}$, so D-S.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":100,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the stress-strain behaviour of the elastic tissue of the aorta is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Its elastic region is very large","It obeys Hooke's law over most of its elastic region","It has no well-defined plastic region","It belongs to the class of materials called elastomers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: aorta tissue can undergo large strains and still recover.\n(2) Incorrect: NCERT notes that, although the elastic region is large, the tissue does NOT obey Hooke's law over most of it. Its curve is non-linear.\n(3) Correct: there is no well-defined plastic region.\n(4) Correct: materials like aorta tissue and rubber, which can be stretched to large strains, are called elastomers.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":101,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A copper wire of length 1.5 m and a steel wire of length 1.0 m, both of the same diameter, are joined end to end and hang vertically supporting a load. Take $Y_{copper} = 1.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$ and $Y_{steel} = 2.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$, and neglect the masses of the wires. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The tensile stress is the same in both wires.\n(ii) The strain in copper is twice the strain in steel.\n(iii) The elongation of copper is three times that of steel.\n(iv) The elastic potential energy stored in steel is three times that stored in copper.\n(v) The elastic energy per unit volume in copper is twice that in steel.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the tension and area are the same, so $\\sigma$ is equal in both.\n(ii) Correct: $\\varepsilon = \\sigma/Y$, so $\\varepsilon_c/\\varepsilon_s = Y_s/Y_c = 2$.\n(iii) Correct: $\\Delta L_c/\\Delta L_s = 2\\times\\frac{1.5}{1.0} = 3$.\n(iv) Incorrect: $U = \\frac{1}{2}F\\Delta L$ with the same $F$, so copper stores three times as much as steel, not the reverse.\n(v) Correct: $u = \\frac{1}{2}\\sigma\\varepsilon$ with the same $\\sigma$, so the ratio is 2.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":102,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Although the defining relation $B = -\\frac{p}{\\Delta V/V}$ has a negative sign, the bulk modulus of a material in equilibrium is always a positive quantity.\nReason (R): When the pressure on a body is increased, its volume decreases, so $\\Delta V$ is negative when $p$ is positive.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that B is always positive for a system in equilibrium.\nR is true: an increase in pressure produces a decrease in volume, so $\\Delta V < 0$ when $p > 0$.\nThe ratio $\\frac{p}{\\Delta V/V}$ is therefore negative. The minus sign in the definition makes B positive, so R directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":103,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A weightlifter stands upright on both legs and holds a 40 kg barbell overhead. The extra load is shared equally by his two thighbones. Each thighbone has a length of 45 cm and an effective radius of 1.5 cm, and the Young's modulus of bone is $9.4 \\times 10^{9}$ N $m^{-2}$. By how much is each thighbone compressed because of the barbell? (g = 10 m/$s^2$)","image":"","options":["13.5 $\\mu m$","27.1 $\\mu m$","3.4 $\\mu m$","54.2 $\\mu m$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: extra weight = 40 × 10 = 400 N, so F = 200 N on each bone. L = 0.45 m, r = 0.015 m.\nArea: $A = \\pi r^2 = 7.07 \\times 10^{-4}$ $m^2$.\nFormula: $\\Delta L = \\frac{FL}{YA}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta L = \\frac{200 \\times 0.45}{9.4 \\times 10^{9} \\times 7.07 \\times 10^{-4}} = 1.35 \\times 10^{-5}$ m.\nSo $\\Delta L$ ≈ 13.5 $\\mu m$. Option (2) comes from forgetting to share the load between the two bones.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":104,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Two wires A and B are made of the same material and carry the same load. Wire B is twice as long as A and has twice the diameter of A. What is the ratio of the elongation of B to that of A ($\\frac{\\Delta L_B}{\\Delta L_A}$)?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{2}$","2","$\\frac{1}{4}$","1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: same Y and same F; $L_B = 2L_A$, $d_B = 2d_A$.\nFormula: $\\Delta L = \\frac{FL}{AY}$ and $A \\propto d^2$, so $\\Delta L \\propto \\frac{L}{d^2}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{\\Delta L_B}{\\Delta L_A} = \\frac{2}{2^2} = \\frac{2}{4}$.\nSo the ratio = $\\frac{1}{2}$. Taking area proportional to diameter, instead of diameter squared, would wrongly give 1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":105,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"The shear modulus of a material is defined as the ratio of:","image":"","options":["tangential force to the area of the face on which it acts","shearing stress to shearing strain; it is also called the modulus of rigidity","shearing strain to shearing stress; it is also called compressibility","hydraulic stress to volume strain; it is also called the bulk modulus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines G as shearing stress divided by the corresponding shearing strain. Another name for G is the modulus of rigidity.\nOption (1) gives only the shearing stress, not a modulus.\nOption (3) inverts the ratio. Compressibility is 1/B, not related to shear.\nOption (4) is the definition of the bulk modulus B.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":106,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are examples of purely elastic deformation, where the body fully regains its original size and shape once the force is removed?\n(i) A helical spring gently pulled at its ends and released\n(ii) A lump of putty squeezed in the hand\n(iii) A wet mud ball pressed flat\n(iv) A rubber band moderately stretched and released\n(v) A steel wire loaded within its proportional (linear) region and unloaded\n(vi) A metal wire loaded to a point between its yield point and ultimate tensile strength and then unloaded","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Elastic: the spring returns to its original length.\n(ii) Plastic: putty stays deformed.\n(iii) Plastic: mud stays deformed.\n(iv) Elastic: rubber is an elastomer and regains its shape.\n(v) Elastic: within the linear region the wire fully recovers.\n(vi) Not elastic: beyond the yield point the wire keeps a permanent set, so its strain is not zero even at zero stress.\nCount = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":107,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A wire has original length $L$ and original diameter $d$. Under a load, it elongates by $\\Delta L$ and its diameter contracts by $\\Delta d$. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Longitudinal strain\n(B) Lateral strain\n(C) Poisson's ratio\n(D) Dimensional formula of Poisson's ratio\nList-II\n(I) $\\frac{\\Delta d}{d}$\n(II) $\\frac{\\Delta d}{\\Delta L} \\times \\frac{L}{d}$\n(III) $[M^0L^0T^0]$\n(IV) $\\frac{\\Delta L}{L}$","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Longitudinal strain is the change in length over the original length, $\\frac{\\Delta L}{L}$, so A-IV.\n(B) Lateral strain is the change in diameter over the original diameter, $\\frac{\\Delta d}{d}$, so B-I.\n(C) Poisson's ratio is $\\frac{\\Delta d/d}{\\Delta L/L} = \\frac{\\Delta d}{\\Delta L} \\times \\frac{L}{d}$, so C-II.\n(D) It is a ratio of two strains, so it is dimensionless, $[M^0L^0T^0]$: D-III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":108,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel wire ($Y = 2 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$) is 3 m long and has a cross-sectional area of 2 mm$^2$. It is stretched within its elastic limit until it stores 0.6 J of elastic potential energy. What is the tension in the wire at that moment?","image":"","options":["800 N","200 N","1200 N","400 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $Y = 2 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$, $L = 3$ m, $A = 2 \\times 10^{-6}$ m$^2$, $U = 0.6$ J.\nFrom $U = \\frac{YAl^2}{2L}$: $l^2 = \\frac{2UL}{YA} = \\frac{3.6}{4 \\times 10^{5}} = 9 \\times 10^{-6}$, so $l = 3 \\times 10^{-3}$ m.\nTension: $F = \\frac{YAl}{L} = \\frac{2 \\times 10^{11} \\times 2 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 3 \\times 10^{-3}}{3}$\nSo $F = 400$ N.\nCheck: $\\frac{1}{2}Fl = 0.5 \\times 400 \\times 0.003 = 0.6$ J ✓\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":109,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which of two materials is considered 'more elastic'?","image":"","options":["The one that stretches more for a given load","The one that stretches less for a given load","The one with the smaller Young's modulus","The one that can be pulled to several times its length, like rubber"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In daily life we often call a material that stretches more 'more elastic'. NCERT points out that this is a misnomer.\nA material that stretches less for a given load is the more elastic one, and it has a larger Young's modulus.\nThis is why steel is more elastic than rubber, even though rubber stretches far more. Options (1), (3) and (4) all describe the popular misconception.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":110,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["Real solids are perfectly rigid and do not change shape under any applied force.","A rigid body is an idealisation; even a stiff steel bar deforms when a sufficiently large force acts on it.","A lump of putty regains its original shape once the deforming force is removed.","A restoring force develops in a body only when its deformation is visible to the eye."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: real solids can be stretched, compressed and bent, so none is perfectly rigid.\n(2) Correct: NCERT notes that even an appreciably rigid steel bar deforms under a large enough force, so rigidity is an idealisation.\n(3) Incorrect: putty is nearly an ideal plastic and stays permanently deformed.\n(4) Incorrect: a deformation may not be noticeable, but it is still present and produces a restoring force.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":111,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Stress is defined as the restoring force developed per unit area of a body. What is the dimensional formula of stress?","image":"","options":["$[MLT^{-2}]$","$[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$","$[ML^{2}T^{-2}]$","$[M^{0}L^{0}T^{0}]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Stress = force/area.\nForce has dimensions $[MLT^{-2}]$ and area has dimensions $[L^{2}]$.\nSo stress has dimensions $[MLT^{-2}]/[L^{2}] = [ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$. This is the same as pressure, and its SI unit is the pascal ($N\\,m^{-2}$).\n$[MLT^{-2}]$ is the dimension of force alone. $[M^{0}L^{0}T^{0}]$ belongs to strain, which is a dimensionless ratio.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":112,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"On the typical stress-strain curve of a metal, O is the origin, A is the end of the linear region, B is the yield point, C is a point between B and D, D is the point of maximum stress and E is the fracture point. Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Hooke's law is obeyed in the region O to A","Between A and B, stress is not proportional to strain, yet the body regains its original dimensions on unloading","If the load is removed at C, the strain returns to zero along with the stress","If points D and E are close together, the material is brittle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the region O to A is linear, so Hooke's law holds there.\n(2) Correct: the region A to B is non-linear but still elastic, so the body recovers on unloading.\n(3) Incorrect: C lies beyond the yield point. On unloading at C the strain does not become zero, and the material acquires a permanent set (plastic deformation).\n(4) Correct: when the ultimate strength point D and the fracture point E are close, the material is brittle.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":113,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (feature of the stress-strain curve of a metal) with List-II (what it signifies).\nList-I:\n(P) Region OA\n(Q) Point B\n(R) Unloading from a point C between B and D\n(S) Point E\nList-II:\n(1) Fracture\n(2) Stress proportional to strain\n(3) Permanent set\n(4) Yield strength\nChoose the correct matching.","image":"","options":["P-2, Q-4, R-3, S-1","P-2, Q-3, R-4, S-1","P-4, Q-2, R-3, S-1","P-2, Q-4, R-1, S-3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"P: OA is the linear region, so stress ∝ strain → (2).\nQ: B is the yield point (elastic limit), and its stress is the yield strength $\\sigma_y$ → (4).\nR: On unloading from C, the strain is not zero even when the stress becomes zero. This is a permanent set (plastic deformation) → (3).\nS: Fracture occurs at E → (1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":114,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which of the following correctly gives the approximate Poisson's ratio for steels and for aluminium alloys?","image":"","options":["Steels: about 0.33; aluminium alloys: 0.28 to 0.30","Steels: 2.8 to 3.0; aluminium alloys: about 3.3","Steels: 0.28 to 0.30; aluminium alloys: about 0.33","Steels: 0.50; aluminium alloys: 0.10"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that Poisson's ratio for steels lies between 0.28 and 0.30.\nFor aluminium alloys, NCERT gives a value of about 0.33.\nOption (1) swaps the two materials.\nOption (2) is ten times too large. For these materials the lateral strain is smaller than the longitudinal strain, so the ratio must be less than 1.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":115,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A rectangular beam supported near its ends and loaded at the centre sags by $\\delta = \\frac{Wl^3}{4bd^3Y}$. If the depth $d$ of the beam is doubled and its breadth $b$ is halved, keeping the load, span and material the same, the sag becomes:","image":"","options":["one-fourth of the original","one-half of the original","twice the original","four times the original"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $d' = 2d$, $b' = b/2$; $W$, $l$ and $Y$ are unchanged.\nFormula: $\\delta \\propto \\frac{1}{bd^3}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{\\delta'}{\\delta} = \\frac{bd^3}{(b/2)(2d)^3} = \\frac{1}{4}$.\nSo the sag becomes one-fourth of the original. This shows that increasing depth is far more effective than increasing breadth.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":116,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel wire with Poisson's ratio 0.30 is stretched so that its longitudinal strain is $1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$. Treating the changes as small, what is the percentage increase in the volume of the wire?","image":"","options":["0.04 %","0.16 %","0.07 %","0.10 %"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\varepsilon = \\frac{\\Delta L}{L} = 1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$, Poisson's ratio $\\sigma = 0.30$.\nFormula: $V = \\frac{\\pi d^2 L}{4}$, so for small changes $\\frac{\\Delta V}{V} = \\frac{\\Delta L}{L} + 2\\frac{\\Delta d}{d}$.\nThe diameter decreases, so $\\frac{\\Delta d}{d} = -\\sigma\\varepsilon$. This gives $\\frac{\\Delta V}{V} = \\varepsilon(1 - 2\\sigma)$.\nSubstituting: $10^{-3} \\times (1 - 0.60) = 4 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nSo the volume increases by 0.04 %. The value 0.16 % comes from wrongly adding the lateral strain.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":117,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A metal wire of original length 2.0 m is stretched by a load. Its length increases by 1.0 mm. What is the longitudinal strain in the wire?","image":"","options":["$2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$","$2.0 \\times 10^{-4}$","$5.0 \\times 10^{-4}$","$5.0 \\times 10^{-3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $L = 2.0$ m, $\\Delta L = 1.0$ mm $= 1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m.\nFormula: longitudinal strain $= \\frac{\\Delta L}{L}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1.0 \\times 10^{-3}}{2.0} = 5.0 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nSo the strain is $5.0 \\times 10^{-4}$, a pure number with no unit.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":118,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Four identical hollow cylindrical steel columns uniformly share the weight of a structure of mass 48,000 kg. Each column has an inner radius of 20 cm and an outer radius of 40 cm. What is the compressive stress in each column? (g = 10 m s$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["$2.39 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa","$12.7 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa","$9.55 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa","$3.18 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: total weight = 48000 × 10 = $4.8 \\times 10^{5}$ N. Each column carries one quarter, i.e. $1.2 \\times 10^{5}$ N.\nFormula: stress = F/A, where the load-bearing area is $A = \\pi(r_2^2 - r_1^2)$.\nSubstituting: $A = \\pi(0.16 - 0.04) = 0.377$ m$^2$, so stress = $1.2 \\times 10^{5}/0.377$.\nSo stress ≈ $3.18 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa.\nUsing only the outer area gives option (1), which is wrong.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":119,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Wires A and B are made of the same material and carry the same tensile load. Wire B is twice as long as A and has twice the radius of A. What is the ratio $\\frac{U_A}{U_B}$ of the elastic potential energies stored in them?","image":"","options":["1/2","4","2","1/4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: same F and Y, $L_B = 2L_A$, $r_B = 2r_A$, so $A_B = 4A_A$.\nFormula: $U = \\frac{1}{2}Fl$ with $l = \\frac{FL}{YA}$, which gives $U = \\frac{F^2L}{2YA}$. Hence $U \\propto \\frac{L}{A}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{U_A}{U_B} = \\frac{L_A/A_A}{2L_A/4A_A} = \\frac{1}{1/2}$\nSo $\\frac{U_A}{U_B} = 2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":120,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"In a circus act, a performer of mass 60 kg lies on his back and supports a human pyramid on his legs. The total mass of everyone and everything in the act, including himself, is 240 kg. Each thighbone is 40 cm long with an effective radius of 2.0 cm. Young's modulus of bone is $9.4 \\times 10^{9}$ N m$^{-2}$. Find the compression of each thighbone. (g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["0.0305 mm","0.0406 mm","0.0610 mm","0.0813 mm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: supported mass = 240 − 60 = 180 kg, so the weight on the legs = 1800 N. Each femur carries 900 N.\nFormula: $\\Delta L = \\frac{FL}{YA}$, with $A = \\pi r^2 = \\pi (0.02)^2 = 1.257 \\times 10^{-3}$ m$^2$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta L = \\frac{900 \\times 0.40}{9.4 \\times 10^{9} \\times 1.257 \\times 10^{-3}} = 3.05 \\times 10^{-5}$ m.\nSo $\\Delta L$ ≈ 0.0305 mm. Forgetting to remove his own mass gives 0.0406 mm; forgetting to share the load between two legs gives 0.0610 mm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":121,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A light wire of cross-sectional area 0.4 mm$^2$ passes over a light frictionless pulley. Blocks of masses 2 kg and 3 kg hang from its two ends, and the system moves freely. Take g = 10 m/s$^2$. What is the tensile stress in the wire during the motion?","image":"","options":["$1.25 \\times 10^{8}$ Pa","$7.5 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa","$6.0 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa","$5.0 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: m$_1$ = 2 kg, m$_2$ = 3 kg, A = $4 \\times 10^{-7}$ m$^2$.\nFormula: for the accelerating system, $T = \\frac{2m_1m_2g}{m_1+m_2}$, and stress = $\\frac{T}{A}$.\nSubstituting: T = $\\frac{2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 10}{5}$ = 24 N. The tension is 24 N, not the sum of the weights (50 N).\nSo stress = $\\frac{24}{4 \\times 10^{-7}}$ = $6.0 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":122,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about shear modulus is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The shear modulus of a material is also called its modulus of rigidity","For most materials, the shear modulus is roughly one-third of the Young's modulus","A shearing stress with a definite shear modulus can be set up in liquids as well as in solids","Under a pure shearing stress, a body changes its shape but not its volume"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: G is also called the modulus of rigidity.\n(2) Correct: NCERT notes that G is generally less than Y, with $G \\approx Y/3$ for most materials.\n(3) Incorrect: shearing stress is possible only in solids, because only solids have a definite shape. Young's and shear moduli apply only to solids.\n(4) Correct: Table 8.4 lists shear as producing a change in shape with no change in volume.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":123,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A metal wire hanging from a support is stretched by a load at its lower end. Its length increases and its diameter decreases slightly. What is the strain perpendicular to the direction of the applied force called?","image":"","options":["Shearing strain","Volume strain","Lateral strain","Longitudinal strain"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT calls the strain measured perpendicular to the applied force the lateral strain. For a wire it equals $\\frac{\\Delta d}{d}$.\nLongitudinal strain is $\\frac{\\Delta L}{L}$, measured along the force, so option (4) does not fit.\nShearing strain needs tangential forces, and volume strain needs hydraulic pressure. Neither applies to a hanging wire.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":124,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A copper wire of length 2.0 m and a steel wire of length 1.5 m, each 2.0 mm in diameter, are joined end to end. The combination hangs from a rigid support with a load at the lower end. The total elongation is 0.55 mm. Take $Y_{Cu} = 1.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$ and $Y_{steel} = 2.0 \\times 10^{11}$ N m$^{-2}$, and neglect the weights of the wires. Find the load.","image":"","options":["62.8 N","115 N","251 N","31.4 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: both wires have the same tension $W$ and the same area $A = \\pi(10^{-3})^2$ m$^2$, so the stress is the same in each.\nFormula: $\\Delta L = \\frac{WL}{AY}$, so $\\frac{\\Delta L_c}{\\Delta L_s} = \\frac{Y_s}{Y_c} \\cdot \\frac{L_c}{L_s} = 2 \\times \\frac{2.0}{1.5} = \\frac{8}{3}$.\nWith $\\Delta L_c + \\Delta L_s = 0.55$ mm, we get $\\Delta L_s = 0.15$ mm and $\\Delta L_c = 0.40$ mm.\nSubstituting: $W = \\frac{AY_s \\Delta L_s}{L_s} = \\frac{\\pi \\times 10^{-6} \\times 2 \\times 10^{11} \\times 1.5 \\times 10^{-4}}{1.5} = 20\\pi$.\nSo $W \\approx 62.8$ N. Splitting the elongation equally (115 N) or using the diameter as the radius (251 N) gives wrong answers.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":125,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A lake trench has a depth of 2000 m. Find the fractional compression $\\Delta V/V$ of water at its bottom due to the water column above it. Take the density of water as 1000 kg $m^{-3}$, bulk modulus of water as $2.2 \\times 10^9$ N $m^{-2}$, and g = 10 m $s^{-2}$. Ignore atmospheric pressure.","image":"","options":["0.91 %","0.45 %","1.36 %","9.1 %"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: h = 2000 m, ρ = 1000 kg $m^{-3}$, g = 10 m $s^{-2}$, B = $2.2\\times10^9$ Pa.\nFormula: p = hρg and ΔV/V = p/B.\nSubstituting: p = 2000 × 1000 × 10 = $2\\times10^7$ Pa.\nΔV/V = $2\\times10^7/2.2\\times10^9$ = $9.1\\times10^{-3}$.\nSo the fractional compression = 0.91 %.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":126,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Poisson's ratio is the ratio of lateral strain to longitudinal strain in a stretched wire, and for steels its value lies between 0.28 and 0.30.\nStatement II: For a steel wire, Poisson's ratio doubles if the original length of the wire is doubled, the material remaining the same.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. Poisson's ratio is $\\frac{\\Delta d/d}{\\Delta L/L}$, and NCERT gives 0.28–0.30 for steels (about 0.33 for aluminium alloys).\nStatement II is false. Poisson's ratio is a pure number that depends only on the nature of the material, not on the dimensions of the wire. Doubling the length doubles $\\Delta L$ for the same load, but both strains, and hence their ratio, stay the same.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":127,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"An aluminium cube of edge 10 cm and a copper cube of edge 5 cm each have their bottom face fixed. The same tangential force F is applied to the top face of each. Take G(aluminium) = 25 GPa and G(copper) = 42 GPa. What is the ratio of the sideways displacement of the top face of the aluminium cube to that of the copper cube?","image":"","options":["0.84","1.68","0.42","3.36"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: L(Al) = 0.10 m, L(Cu) = 0.05 m, the same F, G(Al) = 25 GPa and G(Cu) = 42 GPa.\nFormula: $\\Delta x = \\frac{FL}{AG}$. For a cube $A = L^2$, so $\\Delta x = \\frac{F}{LG}$.\nRatio: $\\frac{\\Delta x_{Al}}{\\Delta x_{Cu}} = \\frac{L_{Cu}G_{Cu}}{L_{Al}G_{Al}} = \\frac{0.05 \\times 42}{0.10 \\times 25}$.\nSo the ratio $= \\frac{2.1}{2.5} = 0.84$.\nTrap: ignoring the different edge lengths gives 1.68.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":128,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Steel is considered more elastic than rubber.\nReason (R): A material that stretches to a lesser extent for a given load is considered more elastic.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. In physics, the material that stretches less under a given load is the more elastic one. Calling a material that stretches more 'more elastic' is an everyday misnomer.\nA is true. Steel has a much larger Young's modulus than rubber, so for the same load it stretches far less.\nR is the very criterion that makes A true, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":129,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following units is NOT equivalent to the SI unit of stress?","image":"","options":["N m$^{-2}$","J m$^{-3}$","kg m$^{-1}$ s$^{-2}$","N m$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The SI unit of stress is N m$^{-2}$, called the pascal (Pa).\nJ m$^{-3}$ = (N m)/m$^3$ = N m$^{-2}$, so it is equivalent.\nkg m$^{-1}$ s$^{-2}$ = (kg m s$^{-2}$)/m$^2$ = N m$^{-2}$, so it is equivalent.\nN m$^{-1}$ is force per unit length, which is the unit of surface tension or spring constant. It is not stress.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":130,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A pillar with distributed (broadened) ends can support more load than a pillar with rounded ends.\nStatement II: The design of a bridge or building needs to consider only the Young's modulus of the material used.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT states that a pillar with rounded ends supports less load than one with distributed ends.\nStatement II is incorrect: the precise design must also consider the working conditions, the cost, the long-term reliability of the material, and so on.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":131,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about a wire stretched within its elastic limit are correct?\n(i) Work is done against the interatomic forces of the wire.\n(ii) The work done is stored in the wire as elastic potential energy.\n(iii) For a given material, the energy stored per unit volume is proportional to the square of the strain.\n(iv) If a final tension F produces an extension l, the energy stored is $F \\times l$.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: stretching pulls the atoms apart against their interatomic forces.\n(ii) Correct: this work is stored as elastic potential energy.\n(iii) Correct: $u = \\frac{1}{2}Y\\varepsilon^2$, so $u \\propto \\varepsilon^2$.\n(iv) Incorrect: the tension grows linearly from 0 to F during the stretch. The stored energy is therefore $\\frac{1}{2}Fl$, not $Fl$.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":132,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Compressibility is the reciprocal of bulk modulus, and its SI unit is $Pa^{-1}$ (or $m^2\\,N^{-1}$).\nStatement II: Liquids are more compressible than solids.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. k = 1/B, and B has the unit Pa, so k has the unit $Pa^{-1}$.\nStatement II is correct. Bulk moduli of solids (for example, steel 160 GPa) are much larger than those of liquids (for example, water 2.2 GPa). Liquids therefore have the larger compressibility.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":133,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A rectangular rubber pad of thickness 2.0 cm is fixed at its lower face. A tangential force is applied on its upper face, and the upper face shifts sideways by 1.0 mm relative to the lower face. The angle through which the vertical edge of the pad turns is approximately:","image":"","options":["$0.05^\\circ$","$2.86^\\circ$","$5.0^\\circ$","$0.029^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta x$ = 1.0 mm = $1.0\\times10^{-3}$ m and L = 2.0 cm = $2.0\\times10^{-2}$ m.\nFormula: shearing strain = $\\frac{\\Delta x}{L} = \\tan\\theta \\approx \\theta$ (in radians).\nSubstituting: $\\theta \\approx \\frac{10^{-3}}{2\\times10^{-2}}$ = 0.05 rad.\nConverting to degrees: $0.05\\times\\frac{180}{\\pi} \\approx 2.86^\\circ$. Option (1) is the trap of reading the radian value as degrees.\nSo the angle of shear is about $2.86^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":134,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A deforming force is applied to a solid body, which remains in static equilibrium. The restoring force developed inside the body is:","image":"","options":["Equal in magnitude and in the same direction as the applied force","Larger than the applied force and opposite to it","Equal in magnitude but opposite in direction to the applied force","Zero until the elastic limit is crossed"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"When a body in static equilibrium is deformed, internal restoring forces develop in it.\nNCERT states that the restoring force is equal in magnitude but opposite in direction to the applied force.\nThe restoring force per unit area is called stress.\nThe force cannot be zero, because a restoring force exists even for small deformations within the elastic limit.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":135,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"An aluminium alloy wire has length 1.5 m and diameter 3.0 mm. Its Young's modulus is $7.0 \\times 10^{10}$ N $m^{-2}$ and its Poisson's ratio is 0.33. A tensile force of 210 N is applied along its length. Find the decrease in the diameter of the wire. (Take $\\pi = 3.14$.)","image":"","options":["1.27 $\\mu m$","0.21 $\\mu m$","0.14 $\\mu m$","0.42 $\\mu m$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $F = 210$ N, $r = 1.5 \\times 10^{-3}$ m, $Y = 7.0 \\times 10^{10}$ Pa, Poisson's ratio = 0.33, $d = 3.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ m.\nStress: $\\frac{F}{\\pi r^2} = \\frac{210}{7.07 \\times 10^{-6}} \\approx 2.97 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa.\nLongitudinal strain: $\\frac{\\text{stress}}{Y} \\approx 4.24 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nLateral strain: $0.33 \\times 4.24 \\times 10^{-4} \\approx 1.40 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nDecrease in diameter: $1.40 \\times 10^{-4} \\times 3.0 \\times 10^{-3} \\approx 4.2 \\times 10^{-7}$ m = 0.42 $\\mu m$.\nUsing the radius instead of the diameter gives the trap value 0.21 $\\mu m$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":136,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The base of a mountain is under uniform (bulk) compression, like a body immersed in a fluid.\n(ii) The shear component of stress at the base of a mountain of height h is approximately $h\\rho g$.\n(iii) For the same load and dimensions, a beam made of a material with larger Young's modulus sags less.\n(iv) Doubling the breadth of a beam reduces its sag by the same factor as doubling its depth.\n(v) A crane rope is designed so that its extension stays within the elastic limit, so that the load does not deform it permanently.","image":"","options":["3","2","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: the sides of a mountain are free, so the base is not under uniform compression. It has a shear component.\n(ii) Correct: NCERT takes the shear component as approximately $h\\rho g$.\n(iii) Correct: $\\delta \\propto \\frac{1}{Y}$.\n(iv) Incorrect: doubling b gives 1/2 the sag, while doubling d gives 1/8.\n(v) Correct: this is the basis of the condition $A \\geq \\frac{Mg}{\\sigma_y}$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":137,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Bulk modulus is a relevant elastic constant for solids, liquids and gases.\nStatement II: Shear modulus is relevant for solids as well as liquids, since both have a definite volume.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. Bulk modulus describes a change in volume under uniform pressure. Solids, liquids and gases can all undergo such a change.\nStatement II is false. Shear modulus and Young's modulus apply only to solids, because only solids have definite lengths and shapes. Shearing stress is possible only in solids, and having a definite volume does not give a liquid shear rigidity.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":138,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel cable of circular cross-section with radius 1.0 cm holds up a lift cabin. The stress in the cable must not exceed $2.0 \\times 10^{8}$ N m$^{-2}$. What is the maximum load the cable can support?","image":"","options":["$6.28 \\times 10^{4}$ N","$2.0 \\times 10^{4}$ N","$1.57 \\times 10^{4}$ N","$2.51 \\times 10^{5}$ N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $r = 1.0$ cm $= 10^{-2}$ m, $\\sigma_{max} = 2.0 \\times 10^{8}$ N m$^{-2}$.\nFormula: maximum load $F = \\sigma_{max} A = \\sigma_{max} \\pi r^2$.\nSubstituting: $F = 2.0 \\times 10^{8} \\times 3.14 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nSo $F \\approx 6.28 \\times 10^{4}$ N.\nThe other options come from dropping $\\pi$ ($2.0 \\times 10^{4}$ N), using 0.5 cm as the radius ($1.57 \\times 10^{4}$ N), or using the diameter as the radius ($2.51 \\times 10^{5}$ N).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":139,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following quantities are dimensionless?\nLongitudinal strain, shearing strain, Poisson's ratio, stress, bulk modulus, volume strain, compressibility","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Longitudinal strain ($\\Delta L/L$): dimensionless.\nShearing strain ($\\Delta x/L$ or $\\theta$): dimensionless.\nPoisson's ratio is a ratio of two strains: dimensionless.\nVolume strain ($\\Delta V/V$): dimensionless.\nStress and bulk modulus have dimensions $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$. Compressibility, being $1/B$, has dimensions $[M^{-1}LT^{2}]$.\nTotal dimensionless quantities = 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":140,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When a helical spring is stretched, its extension is governed mainly by the shear modulus of the material of its wire.\nStatement II: For most materials the shear modulus is roughly one-third of the Young's modulus.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. When a coil is stretched, the length of its wire hardly changes. The wire is mainly twisted, which is a shearing deformation, so G controls the extension.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that $G \\approx Y/3$ for most materials, so G is generally less than Y.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":141,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The shear modulus is a meaningful elastic constant only for solids.\nReason (R): Only solids have a definite shape, and shear deformation is a change of shape.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that Young's modulus and shear modulus are relevant only for solids, and that shearing stress is possible only in solids.\nR is true. Shear changes the shape of a body without changing its volume. Only solids have a definite shape that can resist such a change.\nR is the reason G applies only to solids, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":142,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel wire hangs from a rigid ceiling, and a block of mass $M_1$ is attached to its lower end. A brass wire hangs from this block and carries a second block of mass $M_2$ at its lower end. Neglect the masses of the wires. What is the tension in the steel wire?","image":"","options":["$(M_1 + M_2)g$","$M_1 g$","$M_2 g$","$2(M_1 + M_2)g$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Consider the system of both blocks and the brass wire below the steel wire.\nThe steel wire must support the total weight below it, which is $(M_1 + M_2)g$.\nThe brass wire carries only $M_2 g$, so $M_2 g$ is the tension in the brass wire, not the steel wire.\nThe ceiling pulls up with an equal force, but the tension at any cross-section is still $(M_1+M_2)g$, not twice this value (NCERT, Points to Ponder 1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":143,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A beam supported near its ends and loaded at the centre sags by $\\delta$. The beam is replaced by another beam of the same material and same span. The new beam has double the depth and half the breadth, and the same load is applied. The new sag is:","image":"","options":["$\\delta/4$","$\\delta/2$","$\\delta$","$\\delta/16$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $d' = 2d$, $b' = b/2$; same $W$, $l$ and $Y$.\nFormula: $\\delta = \\frac{Wl^3}{4bd^3Y}$, so $\\delta \\propto \\frac{1}{bd^3}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{\\delta'}{\\delta} = \\frac{b\\,d^3}{(b/2)(2d)^3} = \\frac{1}{4}$.\nSo the new sag is $\\delta/4$. Increasing depth reduces bending much more than breadth does.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":144,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a body in equilibrium under hydraulic compression, the bulk modulus is always positive, even though its defining relation carries a negative sign.\nReason (R): An increase in pressure on the body produces a decrease in its volume.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. $B = -\\frac{p}{\\Delta V/V}$, and NCERT states that B is always positive for a system in equilibrium.\nR is true. A positive pressure increase makes $\\Delta V$ negative.\nR explains A. Because $\\Delta V$ is negative, the minus sign in the formula makes B positive.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":145,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A wire hangs from a ceiling, and a weight F is suspended from its lower end.\nStatement I: The tension at any cross-section of the wire is 2F, because the ceiling also pulls the wire with a force F.\nStatement II: Stress is a vector quantity, since it is force per unit area.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The ceiling's pull balances the weight, but the tension at any cross-section is just F, not 2F. So the tensile stress is F/A.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT notes that stress cannot be assigned a specific direction, so it is not a vector. Only the force on a chosen side of a section has a definite direction.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":146,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a beam of a given material, length and load, increasing its depth is more effective in reducing its sag than increasing its breadth by the same factor.\nReason (R): The sag of a centrally loaded beam is proportional to $d^{-3}$ but only to $b^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: from $\\delta = \\frac{Wl^3}{4bd^3Y}$, we get $\\delta \\propto d^{-3}$ and $\\delta \\propto b^{-1}$.\nA is true: doubling d cuts the sag to 1/8, while doubling b cuts it only to 1/2.\nThe stronger power of d is exactly the reason depth is more effective, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":147,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Steel is considered more elastic than rubber.\nReason (R): For the same load, a material with a larger Young's modulus stretches by a smaller amount, and the Young's modulus of steel is much larger than that of rubber.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT says a material that stretches less for a given load is more elastic. The everyday idea that 'stretches more means more elastic' is a misnomer.\nR is true: from $Y = \\frac{FL}{A\\Delta L}$, a larger $Y$ gives a smaller $\\Delta L$ for the same $F$, $L$ and $A$. Steel has a very large $Y$ compared with rubber.\nR directly explains why steel counts as more elastic.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":148,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following quantities are dimensionless (have no unit)?\nLongitudinal strain, shearing strain, Poisson's ratio, compressibility, stress, volume strain","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Longitudinal strain ($\\Delta L/L$): dimensionless.\nShearing strain ($\\Delta x/L = \\tan\\theta$): dimensionless.\nPoisson's ratio is a ratio of two strains, so it is dimensionless.\nCompressibility $k = 1/B$ has the unit $\\text{Pa}^{-1}$, so it is not dimensionless.\nStress is measured in $\\text{N m}^{-2}$, so it is not dimensionless.\nVolume strain ($\\Delta V/V$): dimensionless.\nTotal = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":149,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A copper block is sheared so that its vertical faces turn through an angle $\\theta = 2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ rad. The shear modulus of copper is 42 GPa. What is the shearing stress in the block?","image":"","options":["$2.1 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa","$4.2 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa","$8.4 \\times 10^{6}$ Pa","$8.4 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $G = 42 \\times 10^{9}$ Pa and $\\theta = 2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ rad.\nFormula: $\\sigma_s = G\\theta$\nSubstituting: $\\sigma_s = 42 \\times 10^{9} \\times 2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$\nSo $\\sigma_s = 8.4 \\times 10^{7}$ Pa.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":150,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If a metal wire is loaded to a point between A (end of linear region) and B (yield point) and then unloaded, it returns to its original length.\nStatement II: Once the stress exceeds the yield strength, the strain increases only slowly even for a large increase in stress.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In region AB stress and strain are not proportional, but the body still regains its original dimensions when the load is removed. B is the elastic limit.\nStatement II is incorrect. Beyond the yield strength, strain increases rapidly even for a small change in stress (region B to D). It does not increase slowly.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":151,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A rectangular beam supported near its ends and loaded at the centre sags by 4 mm. The beam is replaced by another beam of the same material and same span, carrying the same load. The new beam has twice the breadth and half the depth of the original. What is the new sag at the centre?","image":"","options":["16 mm","4 mm","1 mm","8 mm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\delta_1 = 4$ mm, $b_2 = 2b$, $d_2 = d/2$. Load, span and $Y$ are unchanged.\nFormula: $\\delta = \\frac{Wl^3}{4bd^3Y}$, so $\\delta \\propto \\frac{1}{bd^3}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{\\delta_2}{\\delta_1} = \\frac{bd^3}{(2b)(d/2)^3} = \\frac{1}{2 \\times \\frac{1}{8}} = 4$.\nSo $\\delta_2 = 4 \\times 4 = 16$ mm.\nThis shows that depth matters far more than breadth in resisting bending.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":152,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A metal cube is placed in a fluid under high pressure. Each edge decreases by 0.01% while the cube keeps its shape. What is the approximate percentage decrease in its volume?","image":"","options":["0.01%","0.03%","0.003%","0.0001%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: fractional change in each edge $= 10^{-4}$, and the shape is unchanged (hydraulic compression).\nFormula: $V = a^3$, so $\\frac{\\Delta V}{V} \\approx 3\\frac{\\Delta a}{a}$ for small changes.\nSubstituting: $3 \\times 0.01\\% = 0.03\\%$.\nSo the volume strain is about 0.03%. The option 0.01% wrongly treats volume strain as equal to the edge strain.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":153,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"In Hooke's law written as stress = $k$ × strain, the dimensional formula of the modulus of elasticity $k$ is","image":"","options":["$[MLT^{-2}]$","$[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$","$[ML^{2}T^{-2}]$","dimensionless"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Strain is a ratio of two like quantities, so it is dimensionless.\nTherefore $k$ = stress/strain has the same dimensions as stress.\nStress = force/area = $[MLT^{-2}]/[L^2] = [ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$, with SI unit N m$^{-2}$ (Pa).\n$[MLT^{-2}]$ is force, not stress.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":154,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A mass M hangs from a vertical wire. It is lowered slowly by hand until the hand no longer supports it, and the wire comes to equilibrium with extension l.\nStatement I: The elastic potential energy stored in the wire is $\\frac{1}{2}Mgl$, while the gravitational potential energy lost by the mass is $Mgl$.\nStatement II: The remaining $\\frac{1}{2}Mgl$ is also stored in the wire, so the total elastic energy in the wire equals $Mgl$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. At equilibrium the tension is $F = Mg$. The tension grew linearly from zero, so the stored energy is $\\frac{1}{2}Fl = \\frac{1}{2}Mgl$. The mass falls by l, so it loses $Mgl$ of gravitational PE.\nStatement II is false. The other $\\frac{1}{2}Mgl$ is absorbed as negative work done by the supporting hand. It is not stored in the wire. The wire's energy depends only on its strain: $\\frac{1}{2}Fl$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":155,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about Poisson's ratio are correct?\n(i) It is the ratio of two strains.\n(ii) Its SI unit is N $m^{-2}$.\n(iii) Within the elastic limit, lateral strain is directly proportional to longitudinal strain.\n(iv) For aluminium alloys its value is about 0.33.\n(v) For a given material, its value increases with the diameter of the wire.","image":"","options":["2","4","5","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: it is lateral strain divided by longitudinal strain.\n(ii) Incorrect: a ratio of two strains is a pure number with no unit.\n(iii) Correct: this is Poisson's observation within the elastic limit.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT gives about 0.33 for aluminium alloys.\n(v) Incorrect: the value depends only on the nature of the material, not on the wire's size.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":156,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Young's modulus\n(B) Shear modulus\n(C) Bulk modulus\n(D) Poisson's ratio\nList-II\n(i) Change in volume with no change in shape\n(ii) Ratio of lateral strain to longitudinal strain\n(iii) Change in shape with no change in volume\n(iv) Change in length under tensile or compressive stress","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Young's modulus relates tensile or compressive stress to longitudinal strain, so A matches (iv).\n(B) Shear modulus involves pure shear, which changes shape but not volume, so B matches (iii).\n(C) Bulk modulus involves hydraulic stress, which changes volume but not shape, so C matches (i).\n(D) Poisson's ratio is lateral strain divided by longitudinal strain, so D matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":157,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel wire is stretched within its elastic limit, producing a longitudinal strain of $1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$. Poisson's ratio for this steel is 0.30. Working to first order in small strains, what is the fractional change in the volume of the wire?","image":"","options":["$1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ increase","$4.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ increase","$4.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ decrease","$1.6 \\times 10^{-3}$ increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\frac{\\Delta L}{L} = 10^{-3}$. Poisson's ratio gives the lateral strain: $\\frac{\\Delta d}{d} = 0.30 \\times 10^{-3}$, and the diameter contracts.\nFormula: $V \\propto d^2L$, so $\\frac{\\Delta V}{V} = \\frac{\\Delta L}{L} - 2\\frac{\\Delta d}{d}$.\nSubstituting: $10^{-3} - 2(0.3 \\times 10^{-3}) = 4.0 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nThe result is positive, so the volume increases by $4.0 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":158,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about strain is correct?","image":"","options":["Longitudinal strain $\\frac{\\Delta L}{L}$ has the SI unit $m^{-1}$ because it is measured per unit length.","Shearing strain is the ratio of the relative displacement $\\Delta x$ of opposite faces to the area of those faces.","A solid sphere under uniform hydraulic pressure shows a volume strain $\\frac{\\Delta V}{V}$ with no change in its geometrical shape.","Strain has the same dimensional formula as stress, $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: $\\Delta L$ and $L$ are both lengths, so their ratio has no unit.\n(2) Incorrect: shearing strain is $\\frac{\\Delta x}{L}$, the displacement divided by the length of the body, not by an area.\n(3) Correct: hydraulic pressure acts normally at every point of the surface. The volume decreases but the shape stays the same.\n(4) Incorrect: $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$ is the dimensional formula of stress. Strain is a ratio of like quantities, so it is dimensionless.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":159,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Two cubes A and B are made of the same material. Their edges are $a$ and $2a$ respectively. Each has its bottom face fixed. The same tangential force F is applied to the top face of each cube. The ratios $\\theta_A/\\theta_B$ (shear angles) and $\\Delta x_A/\\Delta x_B$ (displacements of the top faces) are respectively:","image":"","options":["2 and 4","1 and 2","4 and 8","4 and 2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Shear angle: $\\theta = \\frac{F}{GA} = \\frac{F}{Ga^2}$, so $\\theta \\propto \\frac{1}{a^2}$.\nThis gives $\\frac{\\theta_A}{\\theta_B} = \\frac{(2a)^2}{a^2} = 4$.\nDisplacement: $\\Delta x = \\theta L = \\theta a = \\frac{F}{Ga}$, so $\\Delta x \\propto \\frac{1}{a}$.\nThis gives $\\frac{\\Delta x_A}{\\Delta x_B} = 2$.\nThe smaller cube has 4 times the strain but only twice the displacement, because its height is also half.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":160,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes elastomers such as rubber and the elastic tissue of the aorta?","image":"","options":["They obey Hooke's law throughout their elastic region","Their elastic region is very large, they do not obey Hooke's law over most of it, and they have no well-defined plastic region","They have a small elastic region followed by a long plastic region","They fracture just beyond their linear region, like brittle materials"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT's curve for aorta tissue shows a very large elastic region. Rubber, for example, can be stretched to several times its original length and still regain its shape.\nHowever, Hooke's law is not obeyed over most of that region.\nThere is also no well-defined plastic region.\nOption (1) contradicts the summary statement that elastomers do not obey Hooke's law. Options (3) and (4) contradict the large elastic range.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":161,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which pair of substances is close to ideal plastics?","image":"","options":["Steel and brass","Rubber and aorta tissue","Putty and mud","Glass and concrete"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A plastic substance shows no gross tendency to regain its shape after the force is removed. It stays permanently deformed.\nNCERT names putty and mud as close to ideal plastics.\nRubber and aorta tissue are elastomers. They undergo large strains but still regain their shape, so they are not plastic.\nSteel, brass, glass and concrete all show elastic behaviour for small loads.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":162,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"According to the data in NCERT Table 8.3, which of the following materials is the least compressible?","image":"","options":["Nickel","Steel","Copper","Mercury"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The least compressible material is the one with the largest bulk modulus, since k = 1/B.\nTable 8.3 values: nickel 260 GPa, steel 160 GPa, copper 140 GPa, mercury 25 GPa.\nNickel has the largest B, so it compresses least.\nSteel is a tempting choice because it is known for its large Young's modulus, but its bulk modulus is lower than nickel's.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":163,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a shear angle of $10^\\circ$, taking the shearing strain as $\\theta$ instead of $\\tan\\theta$ introduces an error of only about 1%.\nReason (R): For small angles measured in radians, $\\tan\\theta \\approx \\theta$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. $10^\\circ = 0.1745$ rad and $\\tan 10^\\circ = 0.1763$, so the two differ by about 1%, as NCERT states.\nR is true. For small angles in radians, $\\tan\\theta$ is very nearly equal to $\\theta$.\nThis approximation is exactly why shearing strain $\\frac{\\Delta x}{L} = \\tan\\theta \\approx \\theta$ is acceptable, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":164,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel rod of radius 5 mm is pulled along its length by a force of 6.28 kN. What is the tensile stress in the rod? (Take $\\pi = 3.14$)","image":"","options":["$8.0 \\times 10^{7}$ N m$^{-2}$","$2.0 \\times 10^{7}$ N m$^{-2}$","$3.2 \\times 10^{8}$ N m$^{-2}$","$8.0 \\times 10^{5}$ N m$^{-2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: F = 6280 N, r = 5 mm = $5 \\times 10^{-3}$ m.\nFormula: stress = $\\frac{F}{A} = \\frac{F}{\\pi r^2}$.\nSubstituting: A = $3.14 \\times (5 \\times 10^{-3})^2 = 7.85 \\times 10^{-5}$ m$^2$, so stress = $\\frac{6280}{7.85 \\times 10^{-5}}$.\nSo stress = $8.0 \\times 10^{7}$ N m$^{-2}$. (Using the diameter instead of the radius would give the wrong value, $2.0 \\times 10^7$ N m$^{-2}$.)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":165,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A crane must lift a load of mass 5000 kg without permanently deforming its steel rope. The yield strength of the steel is $2.5 \\times 10^{8}$ N m$^{-2}$. What is the minimum cross-sectional area of the rope? Ignore any safety margin and take g = 10 m/s$^2$.","image":"","options":["2 cm$^2$","0.5 cm$^2$","20 cm$^2$","5 cm$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: M = 5000 kg, $\\sigma_y = 2.5 \\times 10^{8}$ N m$^{-2}$, g = 10 m/s$^2$.\nFormula: the stress must not exceed the yield strength, so $A \\geq \\frac{Mg}{\\sigma_y}$.\nSubstituting: $A = \\frac{5000 \\times 10}{2.5 \\times 10^{8}} = 2 \\times 10^{-4}$ m$^2$.\nConverting: $2 \\times 10^{-4}$ m$^2$ = 2 cm$^2$ (1 m$^2$ = $10^{4}$ cm$^2$).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":166,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) For a wire hanging from a ceiling and carrying a weight W, the tensile stress at a cross-section of area A is $\\frac{2W}{A}$, because the ceiling also pulls the wire with force W.\n(ii) Stress is a vector quantity, since the restoring force producing it has a definite direction.\n(iii) For a body under hydraulic compression, the magnitude of the hydraulic stress equals the hydraulic pressure applied by the fluid.\n(iv) Shearing stress of the kind described for a deformed cylinder is possible only in solids.\n(v) Stress and pressure have the same SI unit and the same dimensional formula.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: the tension at any cross-section is W, not 2W, so the stress is W/A.\n(ii) Incorrect: stress cannot be assigned a specific direction, so it is not a vector. Only the force on one side of a section has a definite direction.\n(iii) Correct: the hydraulic stress equals the applied force per unit area, which is the pressure.\n(iv) Correct: only solids have a definite shape, so only they sustain shear.\n(v) Correct: both are measured in Pa and have dimensions $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":167,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Young's modulus and shear modulus are relevant only for solids.\nReason (R): Only solids have a definite length and a definite shape.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: Young's modulus describes a change in length, and shear modulus describes a change in shape.\nR is true: only solids have a fixed length and a fixed shape. Liquids and gases have only a definite volume or none at all.\nYou cannot define a change in length or shape without a definite length or shape, so R correctly explains A.\nBulk modulus, which involves only volume, applies to all three states of matter.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":168,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Wires of steel and copper with the same cross-sectional area need forces of 2000 N and 1100 N respectively to produce the same strain. Hence steel is more elastic than copper.\nStatement II: For most materials the shear modulus is roughly one-third of the Young's modulus.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Steel needs a larger force for the same strain, so it has the larger Young's modulus. A material that stretches less under a given load is the more elastic one. The everyday idea that stretching more means more elastic is a misnomer.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that G is generally less than Y, with $G \\approx Y/3$ for most materials.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":169,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Putty\n(B) Helical spring stretched gently and released\n(C) Metal wire loaded beyond its yield point and then unloaded\n(D) Rigid body\nList-II\n(i) Idealised solid whose shape never changes\n(ii) Permanent set (plastic deformation)\n(iii) Close to an ideal plastic\n(iv) Elastic deformation\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i","A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-iv","A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Putty shows almost no tendency to regain its shape, so it is close to an ideal plastic: (iii).\n(B) A gently stretched spring regains its length, which is elastic deformation: (iv).\n(C) Beyond the yield point, strain remains even when the load is removed, which is a permanent set: (ii).\n(D) A rigid body is an idealisation of a solid that never deforms: (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":170,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Bulk modulus is relevant for solids, liquids and gases.\n(ii) Hydraulic stress changes both the volume and the shape of a body.\n(iii) For a system in equilibrium, the bulk modulus is always positive.\n(iv) The compressibility of a gas does not depend on its pressure or temperature.\n(v) The hydraulic stress on a body immersed in a fluid is equal in magnitude to the hydraulic pressure.","image":"","options":["3","2","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: bulk modulus describes a volume change, which is possible in all three states of matter.\n(ii) Incorrect: under hydraulic stress the volume changes but the shape does not.\n(iii) Correct: the minus sign in the definition makes B positive.\n(iv) Incorrect: NCERT states that gas compressibilities vary with pressure and temperature.\n(v) Correct: hydraulic stress equals the applied force per unit area, which is the pressure.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":171,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"When a rod is stretched within its elastic limit, its longitudinal strain is $2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ and its lateral strain is $6.0 \\times 10^{-4}$. What is the Poisson's ratio of the material of the rod?","image":"","options":["3.33","0.30","0.60","0.15"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: longitudinal strain = $2.0 \\times 10^{-3}$, lateral strain = $6.0 \\times 10^{-4}$.\nFormula: Poisson's ratio = lateral strain / longitudinal strain.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{6.0 \\times 10^{-4}}{2.0 \\times 10^{-3}} = 0.30$.\nSo Poisson's ratio = 0.30. The value 3.33 comes from inverting the ratio.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":172,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"In the typical stress-strain curve of a metal (Fig. 8.2, points O, A, B, C, D, E in order of increasing strain), over which portion is Hooke's law obeyed?","image":"","options":["From O to A","From O to B","From A to B","From B to D"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Hooke's law (stress ∝ strain) holds only where the curve is linear, which is the region O to A.\nFrom A to B the body is still elastic, so it regains its shape, but stress is no longer proportional to strain.\nSo O to B is the elastic region, not the Hooke's law region. That is why option (2) is the tempting wrong choice.\nB to D is plastic deformation.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":173,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"For the typical stress-strain curve of a metal (O: origin, A: end of linear region, B: yield point, C: a point between B and D, D: ultimate tensile strength, E: fracture), which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Between A and B, stress is not proportional to strain, yet the wire regains its original length on unloading","If the load is removed at C, the strain returns to zero because the material is still elastic","If D and E lie close together, the material is brittle","Beyond D, additional strain is produced even by a reduced applied force"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: up to the elastic limit B the body recovers its shape, even though the region AB is non-linear.\n(2) Incorrect: C lies beyond the yield point. When the load is removed there, the strain stays non-zero (permanent set), which is plastic deformation.\n(3) Correct: D and E close together means the material is brittle; far apart means it is ductile.\n(4) Correct: beyond D, strain increases even with a smaller force until fracture at E.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":174,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Use the NCERT estimate that the shear stress at the base of a mountain is about $h\\rho g$. The rock of a certain region has density $2.5 \\times 10^{3}$ kg m$^{-3}$ and elastic limit $30 \\times 10^{7}$ N m$^{-2}$. What is the maximum possible height of a mountain made of this rock? Take g = 10 m/s$^2$.","image":"","options":["12 km","10 km","1.2 km","120 km"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\rho = 2.5 \\times 10^{3}$ kg m$^{-3}$, elastic limit $= 3 \\times 10^{8}$ N m$^{-2}$, g = 10 m/s$^2$.\nFormula: at the maximum height, $h\\rho g$ equals the elastic limit, so $h = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\rho g}$.\nSubstituting: $h = \\frac{3 \\times 10^{8}}{2.5 \\times 10^{3} \\times 10} = 1.2 \\times 10^{4}$ m.\nSo h = 12 km. (10 km is the NCERT value for a density of $3 \\times 10^{3}$ kg m$^{-3}$.)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-175","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":175,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Gases are about a million times more compressible than solids.\n(ii) The compressibility of a gas has a fixed value that does not depend on its pressure or temperature.\n(iii) Solids are nearly incompressible mainly because neighbouring atoms are tightly coupled.\n(iv) Water has a larger bulk modulus than mercury.\n(v) Bulk modulus is a meaningful elastic constant for solids, liquids and gases.","image":"","options":["3","2","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT states that gases are about a million times more compressible than solids.\n(ii) Incorrect: the compressibility of a gas is large and varies with pressure and temperature.\n(iii) Correct: tight atomic coupling is the main reason solids are nearly incompressible.\n(iv) Incorrect: water has B = 2.2 GPa, while mercury has B = 25 GPa.\n(v) Correct: only volume change is involved, so bulk modulus applies to all three states.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-176","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":176,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation) with List-II (type of stress developed):\nList-I\n(A) A steel rod pulled by equal forces at its two ends along its length\n(B) A pillar supporting the roof of a building\n(C) A book pressed by the palm and pushed horizontally\n(D) A metal ball resting deep at the bottom of the ocean\nList-II\n(I) Hydraulic stress\n(II) Shearing stress\n(III) Tensile stress\n(IV) Compressive stress","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The rod is stretched by normal forces, so it is under tensile stress (III).\n(B) The pillar is squeezed along its length, so it is under compressive stress (IV).\n(C) A tangential force displaces the top face relative to the bottom, so it is under shearing stress (II).\n(D) Water presses normally on every point of the surface, so it is under hydraulic stress (I).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-177","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":177,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Hooke's law is an empirical law.\n(ii) Point B on the stress-strain curve of a metal is called the yield point, also known as the elastic limit.\n(iii) Beyond the ultimate tensile strength point D, additional strain is produced even by a reduced applied force.\n(iv) Hooke's law is obeyed by every material for small deformations.\n(v) Between A and B, stress is proportional to strain.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Hooke's law comes from experiment, not theory. \n(ii) Correct: B is the yield point, or elastic limit. \n(iii) Correct: past D, strain grows even if the force is reduced, until fracture at E. \n(iv) Incorrect: some materials, such as elastomers, do not show a linear relation. \n(v) Incorrect: the proportional region is only OA. Between A and B the curve is not linear. \nThree statements are correct. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-178","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":178,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"For the typical stress-strain curve of a metal (O to A linear, B yield point, D maximum, E fracture), match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(P) Stress at point B\n(Q) Stress at point D\n(R) Portion B to D\n(S) Portion O to A\nList-II:\n(1) Ultimate tensile strength\n(2) Yield strength $\\sigma_y$\n(3) Strain increases rapidly for a small change in stress\n(4) Stress directly proportional to strain","image":"","options":["P-2, Q-1, R-3, S-4","P-1, Q-2, R-3, S-4","P-2, Q-1, R-4, S-3","P-4, Q-1, R-3, S-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"P: The stress at the yield point B is the yield strength $\\sigma_y$, so P-2. \nQ: Point D gives the ultimate tensile strength $\\sigma_u$, so Q-1. \nR: Between B and D, strain increases rapidly even for a small rise in stress, so R-3. \nS: OA is the linear region where Hooke's law holds, so S-4. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-179","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":179,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The SI unit of Young's modulus is the same as that of stress, because strain is a dimensionless quantity.\nStatement II: The dimensional formula of Young's modulus is $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. $Y = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\varepsilon}$, and strain is a ratio of two lengths, so it has no unit. Y therefore has the unit of stress, N m$^{-2}$ or Pa.\nStatement II: Correct. Stress is force per area: $\\frac{[MLT^{-2}]}{[L^2]} = [ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$. Dividing by a dimensionless strain leaves this unchanged.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-SOLIDS-180","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Solids","qno":180,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","type":"mcq","question":"A copper cube of edge 5.0 cm has one face fixed firmly to a vertical wall. A load of mass 420 kg is attached to the opposite face. The shear modulus of copper is 42 GPa. Take g = 10 m/s². What is the vertical deflection of the loaded face?","image":"","options":["$4.0 \\times 10^{-5}$ m","$1.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m","$2.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m","$8.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $F = 420 \\times 10 = 4200$ N, $A = (0.05)^2 = 2.5 \\times 10^{-3}$ m², $L = 0.05$ m, $G = 42 \\times 10^{9}$ Pa.\nFormula: $\\Delta x = \\frac{FL}{AG}$\nSubstituting: $\\Delta x = \\frac{4200 \\times 0.05}{2.5 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 42 \\times 10^{9}}$\nSo $\\Delta x = 2.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ m.\nThe value $4.0 \\times 10^{-5}$ is the shear strain θ, not the displacement.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 8 · Mechanical Properties of Solids","difficulty":"medium"}]});
